{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-1284",
              "name": "Zero-length BIT STRING in X.509 DER causes memcpy with size -1 (remote crash) in TLSe",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Zero-length BIT STRING in X.509 DER causes memcpy with size -1 (remote crash) in TLSe"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy() with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER blob."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy() with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER blob.\n\nA remote unauthenticated peer can terminate the TLS-hosting process. A malicious or MITM server crashes any TLSe client by appending one malformed Certificate message to its ServerHello; a TLSe server crashes when parsing a client certificate in a mutual-TLS deployment. Because the memcpy size is SIZE_MAX the copy always faults at the first unmapped page, so the effect is a reliable per-connection crash rather than a controlled heap write. Applications that load or inspect certificates from files, configuration or an API via tls_certificate_parse() crash on a single crafted blob.\n\nPerform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy() treat every non-positive length as \"no value\" while clearing the destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions.",
                "markdown": "A zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy() with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER blob.\n\nA remote unauthenticated peer can terminate the TLS-hosting process. A malicious or MITM server crashes any TLSe client by appending one malformed Certificate message to its ServerHello; a TLSe server crashes when parsing a client certificate in a mutual-TLS deployment. Because the memcpy size is SIZE_MAX the copy always faults at the first unmapped page, so the effect is a reliable per-connection crash rather than a controlled heap write. Applications that load or inspect certificates from files, configuration or an API via tls_certificate_parse() crash on a single crafted blob.\n\nPerform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy() treat every non-positive length as \"no value\" while clearing the destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-1284",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1284.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-121",
              "name": "Stack buffer overflow in tls_stun_parse() key construction (WebRTC/STUN, pre-auth)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stack buffer overflow in tls_stun_parse() key construction (WebRTC/STUN, pre-auth)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "tls_stun_parse() builds a \"username:realm:password\" string in a 1230-byte stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP datagram triggers it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "tls_stun_parse() builds a \"username:realm:password\" string in a 1230-byte stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP datagram triggers it.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who can send a UDP datagram to a TLSe WebRTC/DTLS peer connection writes up to 47 bytes of fully controlled data past a 1230-byte stack buffer, corrupting the tls_stun_parse() frame. The overwritten region holds other locals of that frame, so the immediate demonstrated effect is corruption of program state in the TLS terminator plus an AddressSanitizer-detected abort; whether the overwrite reaches saved control data depends on the compiler's frame layout, and code execution was not demonstrated. Because the parser runs before any DTLS handshake or STUN authentication, no credential is required.\n\nCompute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm + password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer.",
                "markdown": "tls_stun_parse() builds a \"username:realm:password\" string in a 1230-byte stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP datagram triggers it.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who can send a UDP datagram to a TLSe WebRTC/DTLS peer connection writes up to 47 bytes of fully controlled data past a 1230-byte stack buffer, corrupting the tls_stun_parse() frame. The overwritten region holds other locals of that frame, so the immediate demonstrated effect is corruption of program state in the TLS terminator plus an AddressSanitizer-detected abort; whether the overwrite reaches saved control data depends on the compiler's frame layout, and code execution was not demonstrated. Because the parser runs before any DTLS handshake or STUN authentication, no credential is required.\n\nCompute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm + password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.2",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-121",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/121.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-287",
              "name": "TLS 1.3 client accepts Finished without CertificateVerify, enabling certificate impersonation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "TLS 1.3 client accepts Finished without CertificateVerify, enabling certificate impersonation"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The TLS 1.3 client state machine does not require the server's CertificateVerify message. A server that performs its own key exchange, replays any certificate that satisfies the application's validation callback, and then sends Finished is accepted as authenticated - although it never proved possession of the certificate's private key. Any active man-in-the-middle can therefore impersonate the server to a TLSe client, including for certificates that validate against the configured trust roots."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The TLS 1.3 client state machine does not require the server's CertificateVerify message. A server that performs its own key exchange, replays any certificate that satisfies the application's validation callback, and then sends Finished is accepted as authenticated - although it never proved possession of the certificate's private key. Any active man-in-the-middle can therefore impersonate the server to a TLSe client, including for certificates that validate against the configured trust roots.\n\nComplete loss of server authentication for TLS 1.3 clients: an on-path attacker impersonates any server whose certificate it can obtain (public information), reads and modifies all application traffic, and captures credentials and session data. The application's certificate_verify callback runs and reports success, so the embedding application has no signal that the peer was never authenticated.\n\nRequire the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS 1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of its certificate key.",
                "markdown": "The TLS 1.3 client state machine does not require the server's CertificateVerify message. A server that performs its own key exchange, replays any certificate that satisfies the application's validation callback, and then sends Finished is accepted as authenticated - although it never proved possession of the certificate's private key. Any active man-in-the-middle can therefore impersonate the server to a TLSe client, including for certificates that validate against the configured trust roots.\n\nComplete loss of server authentication for TLS 1.3 clients: an on-path attacker impersonates any server whose certificate it can obtain (public information), reads and modifies all application traffic, and captures credentials and session data. The application's certificate_verify callback runs and reports success, so the embedding application has no signal that the peer was never authenticated.\n\nRequire the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS 1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of its certificate key."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-287",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/287.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-350",
              "name": "DTLS cookie is not bound to the client source address, allowing replay to spoofed peers and amplification",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DTLS cookie is not bound to the client source address, allowing replay to spoofed peers and amplification"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie is computed as HMAC-SHA256(per-process secret, client_random) and carries no information about the client's source address. A cookie obtained once from the attacker's own address is therefore accepted from any other (spoofed) source address as long as the same client_random is replayed, defeating the address-validation purpose of the cookie exchange and enabling a reflected amplification and state-exhaustion primitive."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie is computed as HMAC-SHA256(per-process secret, client_random) and carries no information about the client's source address. A cookie obtained once from the attacker's own address is therefore accepted from any other (spoofed) source address as long as the same client_random is replayed, defeating the address-validation purpose of the cookie exchange and enabling a reflected amplification and state-exhaustion primitive.\n\nAn attacker with one legitimately obtained cookie can make the server perform a full handshake - ephemeral key generation plus certificate and ServerKeyExchange transmission - toward arbitrary spoofed source addresses. This gives about 22x traffic amplification toward third-party victims and unbounded per-address state creation from a small number of packets, with no ability to receive the replies. The address-binding property that the DTLS cookie exchange exists to provide is absent.\n\nBind the cookie to the peer transport address: derive it as HMAC-SHA256(secret, client_random || source address || source port) and verify it against the address the datagram actually arrived from before accepting the ClientHello. Because the library currently has no notion of a peer address, this requires exposing the address to the DTLS layer - for example a tls_set_peer_address(context, addr, addrlen) call that the datagram server invokes before tls_consume_stream, or a callback the library uses when building and verifying the cookie. Additionally, rate-limit cookie issuance per source and avoid generating a full server flight until the cookie has been validated against the address, so a replayed cookie cannot trigger certificate transmission to a third party.",
                "markdown": "The DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie is computed as HMAC-SHA256(per-process secret, client_random) and carries no information about the client's source address. A cookie obtained once from the attacker's own address is therefore accepted from any other (spoofed) source address as long as the same client_random is replayed, defeating the address-validation purpose of the cookie exchange and enabling a reflected amplification and state-exhaustion primitive.\n\nAn attacker with one legitimately obtained cookie can make the server perform a full handshake - ephemeral key generation plus certificate and ServerKeyExchange transmission - toward arbitrary spoofed source addresses. This gives about 22x traffic amplification toward third-party victims and unbounded per-address state creation from a small number of packets, with no ability to receive the replies. The address-binding property that the DTLS cookie exchange exists to provide is absent.\n\nBind the cookie to the peer transport address: derive it as HMAC-SHA256(secret, client_random || source address || source port) and verify it against the address the datagram actually arrived from before accepting the ClientHello. Because the library currently has no notion of a peer address, this requires exposing the address to the DTLS layer - for example a tls_set_peer_address(context, addr, addrlen) call that the datagram server invokes before tls_consume_stream, or a callback the library uses when building and verifying the cookie. Additionally, rate-limit cookie issuance per source and avoid generating a full server flight until the cookie has been validated against the address, so a replayed cookie cannot trigger certificate transmission to a third party."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-350",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/350.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-125",
              "name": "Zero-length CBC record triggers 1-byte heap out-of-bounds read (pt[-1]) in tls_parse_message",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Zero-length CBC record triggers 1-byte heap out-of-bounds read (pt[-1]) in tls_parse_message"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When a zero-length record is processed after a CBC cipher suite has been installed, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and then reads pt[length - 1], i.e. one byte before the allocation. The attacker controls when this happens (any CBC suite plus ChangeCipherSpec), so a single malformed record reaches an out-of-bounds heap read on an unauthenticated connection."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When a zero-length record is processed after a CBC cipher suite has been installed, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and then reads pt[length - 1], i.e. one byte before the allocation. The attacker controls when this happens (any CBC suite plus ChangeCipherSpec), so a single malformed record reaches an out-of-bounds heap read on an unauthenticated connection.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote peer can make the TLS terminator read one byte outside a heap allocation on every such connection attempt. The byte is not returned to the attacker and the record is rejected immediately afterwards, so no data is disclosed; the demonstrated effect is a sanitizer-detected out-of-bounds read that aborts hardened builds, and a small information-free side effect in normal builds. It is a memory-safety defect on a pre-authentication path but its standalone impact is limited.\n\nValidate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally, guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier validation changes.",
                "markdown": "When a zero-length record is processed after a CBC cipher suite has been installed, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and then reads pt[length - 1], i.e. one byte before the allocation. The attacker controls when this happens (any CBC suite plus ChangeCipherSpec), so a single malformed record reaches an out-of-bounds heap read on an unauthenticated connection.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote peer can make the TLS terminator read one byte outside a heap allocation on every such connection attempt. The byte is not returned to the attacker and the record is rejected immediately afterwards, so no data is disclosed; the demonstrated effect is a sanitizer-detected out-of-bounds read that aborts hardened builds, and a small information-free side effect in normal builds. It is a memory-safety defect on a pre-authentication path but its standalone impact is limited.\n\nValidate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally, guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier validation changes."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-125",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/125.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-787",
              "name": "Pre-authentication stack buffer overflow in DTLS fragmented handshake reassembly (TLSe)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pre-authentication stack buffer overflow in DTLS fragmented handshake reassembly (TLSe)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A DTLS peer can overflow a 262,156-byte stack buffer in the TLS record parser by sending a short sequence of handshake fragments whose declared message length does not match the running sum of the fragment lengths. The library accumulates those lengths in `context->dtls_data->fragment->written` without ever comparing the counter against the declared length, then uses the counter as the `memcpy` length when the message is reassembled. No authentication, cookie exchange or prior handshake state is required."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A DTLS peer can overflow a 262,156-byte stack buffer in the TLS record parser by sending a short sequence of handshake fragments whose declared message length does not match the running sum of the fragment lengths. The library accumulates those lengths in `context->dtls_data->fragment->written` without ever comparing the counter against the declared length, then uses the counter as the `memcpy` length when the message is reassembled. No authentication, cookie exchange or prior handshake state is required.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote sender who can reach a TLSe DTLS endpoint writes attacker-controlled bytes past the end of a stack buffer in the process that terminates DTLS. Ten small datagrams reach a precise out-of-bounds write; a few larger fragments turn it into a multi-kilobyte stack smash that crashes the process (observed `SIGSEGV`). Because both the length and the content of the overflow are attacker-chosen, this is a stack memory-corruption primitive — saved registers, return addresses and adjacent locals are all in range — not merely a crash. One malformed datagram sequence is enough to terminate a per-connection server process, so availability impact is total for that process.\n\nEnforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256 KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS handshakes, which the project currently lacks.",
                "markdown": "A DTLS peer can overflow a 262,156-byte stack buffer in the TLS record parser by sending a short sequence of handshake fragments whose declared message length does not match the running sum of the fragment lengths. The library accumulates those lengths in `context->dtls_data->fragment->written` without ever comparing the counter against the declared length, then uses the counter as the `memcpy` length when the message is reassembled. No authentication, cookie exchange or prior handshake state is required.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote sender who can reach a TLSe DTLS endpoint writes attacker-controlled bytes past the end of a stack buffer in the process that terminates DTLS. Ten small datagrams reach a precise out-of-bounds write; a few larger fragments turn it into a multi-kilobyte stack smash that crashes the process (observed `SIGSEGV`). Because both the length and the content of the overflow are attacker-chosen, this is a stack memory-corruption primitive — saved registers, return addresses and adjacent locals are all in range — not merely a crash. One malformed datagram sequence is enough to terminate a per-connection server process, so availability impact is total for that process.\n\nEnforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256 KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS handshakes, which the project currently lacks."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-787",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-295",
              "name": "Chain validation ignores basicConstraints and keyUsage: a non-CA certificate can issue certificates that TLSe reports as verified",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Chain validation ignores basicConstraints and keyUsage: a non-CA certificate can issue certificates that TLSe reports as verified"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "TLSe's chain validation never inspects `basicConstraints` or `keyUsage`. Any certificate whose signature verifies against a chain reaching a loaded trust anchor is accepted as an issuer, including certificates marked `CA:FALSE` and certificates whose key usage excludes `keyCertSign`. A party holding a single trusted end-entity certificate key can therefore mint a certificate for any hostname that TLSe reports as verified, while OpenSSL rejects the same chain."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "TLSe's chain validation never inspects `basicConstraints` or `keyUsage`. Any certificate whose signature verifies against a chain reaching a loaded trust anchor is accepted as an issuer, including certificates marked `CA:FALSE` and certificates whose key usage excludes `keyCertSign`. A party holding a single trusted end-entity certificate key can therefore mint a certificate for any hostname that TLSe reports as verified, while OpenSSL rejects the same chain.\n\nThe bar for impersonating an arbitrary server becomes possession of any single trusted end-entity key instead of a CA key. That converts a leaked, stolen or mis-issued leaf certificate into a universal impersonation capability against TLSe-based clients and servers, and it defeats the purpose of distinguishing CA certificates from leaf certificates in a PKI. It also means subordinate certificates issued with a restricted name scope or `pathLenConstraint` can be used to mint certificates outside that scope.\n\nParse and record the `basicConstraints` extension (OID 2.5.29.19) — the CA boolean and, when present, `pathLenConstraint` — and the `keyUsage` extension (OID 2.5.29.15) `keyCertSign` bit, on every certificate the parser accepts. In the chain walker, require `CA == TRUE` and `keyCertSign` for every certificate used as an issuer of another certificate, and carry a path-length counter that is initialised from the first CA's `pathLenConstraint` and decremented for each intermediate; reject the chain when it is exhausted. Treat a certificate that lacks `basicConstraints` as a non-CA (the default per RFC 5280). Apply the same rule to certificates taken from the supplied chain and to the loaded trust anchors, or document explicitly that anchors are trusted unconditionally.",
                "markdown": "TLSe's chain validation never inspects `basicConstraints` or `keyUsage`. Any certificate whose signature verifies against a chain reaching a loaded trust anchor is accepted as an issuer, including certificates marked `CA:FALSE` and certificates whose key usage excludes `keyCertSign`. A party holding a single trusted end-entity certificate key can therefore mint a certificate for any hostname that TLSe reports as verified, while OpenSSL rejects the same chain.\n\nThe bar for impersonating an arbitrary server becomes possession of any single trusted end-entity key instead of a CA key. That converts a leaked, stolen or mis-issued leaf certificate into a universal impersonation capability against TLSe-based clients and servers, and it defeats the purpose of distinguishing CA certificates from leaf certificates in a PKI. It also means subordinate certificates issued with a restricted name scope or `pathLenConstraint` can be used to mint certificates outside that scope.\n\nParse and record the `basicConstraints` extension (OID 2.5.29.19) — the CA boolean and, when present, `pathLenConstraint` — and the `keyUsage` extension (OID 2.5.29.15) `keyCertSign` bit, on every certificate the parser accepts. In the chain walker, require `CA == TRUE` and `keyCertSign` for every certificate used as an issuer of another certificate, and carry a path-length counter that is initialised from the first CA's `pathLenConstraint` and decremented for each intermediate; reject the chain when it is exhausted. Treat a certificate that lacks `basicConstraints` as a non-CA (the default per RFC 5280). Apply the same rule to certificates taken from the supplied chain and to the loaded trust anchors, or document explicitly that anchors are trusted unconditionally."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-295",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/295.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-476",
              "name": "Stale public-key/signature length after the key is cleared leads to a NULL pointer dereference in tls_certificate_to_string()",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Stale public-key/signature length after the key is cleared leads to a NULL pointer dereference in tls_certificate_to_string()"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`tls_certificate_set_key()` frees the certificate's previous public key through `tls_certificate_set_copy()` but only updates `pk_len` when the new allocation succeeds, so a certificate that sets the key twice — the second time to an empty value — leaves `pk` NULL while `pk_len` keeps the earlier, non-zero length. `tls_certificate_to_string()` then iterates `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len` and dereferences NULL. `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` has the identical defect for `sign_len`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`tls_certificate_set_key()` frees the certificate's previous public key through `tls_certificate_set_copy()` but only updates `pk_len` when the new allocation succeeds, so a certificate that sets the key twice — the second time to an empty value — leaves `pk` NULL while `pk_len` keeps the earlier, non-zero length. `tls_certificate_to_string()` then iterates `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len` and dereferences NULL. `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` has the identical defect for `sign_len`.\n\nAn attacker presenting a certificate with a duplicated or emptied key field causes a NULL pointer dereference in the verifying/certificate-rendering application, aborting the process. The effect is a denial of service against a TLSe peer or server that inspects the certificate it received; there is no data exposure or write primitive.\n\nMake the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference.",
                "markdown": "`tls_certificate_set_key()` frees the certificate's previous public key through `tls_certificate_set_copy()` but only updates `pk_len` when the new allocation succeeds, so a certificate that sets the key twice — the second time to an empty value — leaves `pk` NULL while `pk_len` keeps the earlier, non-zero length. `tls_certificate_to_string()` then iterates `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len` and dereferences NULL. `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` has the identical defect for `sign_len`.\n\nAn attacker presenting a certificate with a duplicated or emptied key field causes a NULL pointer dereference in the verifying/certificate-rendering application, aborting the process. The effect is a denial of service against a TLSe peer or server that inspects the certificate it received; there is no data exposure or write primitive.\n\nMake the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-476",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-294",
              "name": "No DTLS anti-replay window: captured application-data records are re-delivered to the application indefinitely",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "No DTLS anti-replay window: captured application-data records are re-delivered to the application indefinitely"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The DTLS record layer keeps no anti-replay state, so an encrypted application-data record captured off the wire can be re-delivered to the server at any later time and is accepted again: `tls_read()` returns the same plaintext and the application acts on it a second time. The monotonic `tls_write` stream of a TLS connection does not apply to DTLS, and nothing in `tls_consume_stream()`/`tls_read()` tells the application that the record was a duplicate."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The DTLS record layer keeps no anti-replay state, so an encrypted application-data record captured off the wire can be re-delivered to the server at any later time and is accepted again: `tls_read()` returns the same plaintext and the application acts on it a second time. The monotonic `tls_write` stream of a TLS connection does not apply to DTLS, and nothing in `tls_consume_stream()`/`tls_read()` tells the application that the record was a duplicate.\n\nA previously valid DTLS record can be re-injected arbitrarily often and arbitrarily late in the connection, and the receiving application processes it as a fresh message each time. In the demonstrated case one command record was executed three times (once originally, twice by replay) after the peer's sequence had advanced eleven records past it, so no time or sequence proximity is required. Any DTLS-protected operation with side effects is exposed: duplicate transfers or provisioning commands, re-executed alarms or control actions, replayed chat/media-control messages on the library's WebRTC data channels, and double counting in metered or billable flows. Integrity impact scales with what the duplicated message does; confidentiality is unaffected because the replayed ciphertext is unmodified.\n\nImplement the anti-replay window that RFC 6347 section 4.1.2.6 requires for every DTLS context: retain the highest authenticated record sequence number together with a bitmap covering the preceding records (512 records is the usual size), drop any record that is older than the window or whose bit is already set, and advance the window only after the record's AEAD tag has verified so that forged datagrams cannot move it. Sequence numbers must be compared per direction and per epoch, and the window must reset when the epoch advances. Records rejected as duplicates should be discarded silently rather than answered with an alert, so that a replayed datagram does not produce an error response an attacker can use as an oracle. If contexts are exported and imported between processes in the middle of a DTLS association, serialize the window state as well, or document that the receiving process starts with an empty window.",
                "markdown": "The DTLS record layer keeps no anti-replay state, so an encrypted application-data record captured off the wire can be re-delivered to the server at any later time and is accepted again: `tls_read()` returns the same plaintext and the application acts on it a second time. The monotonic `tls_write` stream of a TLS connection does not apply to DTLS, and nothing in `tls_consume_stream()`/`tls_read()` tells the application that the record was a duplicate.\n\nA previously valid DTLS record can be re-injected arbitrarily often and arbitrarily late in the connection, and the receiving application processes it as a fresh message each time. In the demonstrated case one command record was executed three times (once originally, twice by replay) after the peer's sequence had advanced eleven records past it, so no time or sequence proximity is required. Any DTLS-protected operation with side effects is exposed: duplicate transfers or provisioning commands, re-executed alarms or control actions, replayed chat/media-control messages on the library's WebRTC data channels, and double counting in metered or billable flows. Integrity impact scales with what the duplicated message does; confidentiality is unaffected because the replayed ciphertext is unmodified.\n\nImplement the anti-replay window that RFC 6347 section 4.1.2.6 requires for every DTLS context: retain the highest authenticated record sequence number together with a bitmap covering the preceding records (512 records is the usual size), drop any record that is older than the window or whose bit is already set, and advance the window only after the record's AEAD tag has verified so that forged datagrams cannot move it. Sequence numbers must be compared per direction and per epoch, and the window must reset when the epoch advances. Records rejected as duplicates should be discarded silently rather than answered with an alert, so that a replayed datagram does not produce an error response an attacker can use as an oracle. If contexts are exported and imported between processes in the middle of a DTLS association, serialize the window state as well, or document that the receiving process starts with an empty window."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-294",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/294.html"
            },
