      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1284",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Zero-length BIT STRING in X.509 DER causes memcpy with size -1 (remote crash) in TLSe\n\nA zero-length BIT STRING in an X.509 DER structure makes the ASN.1 parser pass a length of -1 into tls_certificate_set_copy(), which calls memcpy() with SIZE_MAX. A TLS peer presenting such a certificate - or any application calling the public tls_certificate_parse() on attacker-supplied DER - crashes the process. The trigger is an 11-byte DER blob."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4597,
                  "endLine": 4601
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Root cause: leading zero stripped without checking that content remains"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4549,
                  "endLine": 4557
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Defence in depth: reject non-positive lengths and never leave a freed pointer behind"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4597,
                        "endLine": 4601
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "void tls_certificate_set_sign_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const unsigned char *val, int len) {\r\n    if ((len > 0) && (!val[0])) {\r\n        val++;\r\n        len--;\r\n    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4549,
                        "endLine": 4557
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "TLS_FREE(*member);\r\n    *member = NULL;\r\n    if (len > 0) {\r\n        *member = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(len + 1);\r\n        if (*member) {\r\n            memcpy(*member, val, len);\r\n            (*member)[len] = 0;\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Perform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy() treat every non-positive length as \"no value\" while clearing the destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions.",
                "markdown": "Perform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy() treat every non-positive length as \"no value\" while clearing the destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "80f06f12bfecbbef9daa0287f862e9b4214a61c1d8773822aa57c9b58ade6ae7"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "1aa1a53acb531b5d361a1013ada18dd89bff45f235906ccd2ce5ae61f34a05ca",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:51:30 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot",
              "cwe": "CWE-1284",
              "impact": "A remote unauthenticated peer can terminate the TLS-hosting process. A malicious or MITM server crashes any TLSe client by appending one malformed Certificate message to its ServerHello; a TLSe server crashes when parsing a client certificate in a mutual-TLS deployment. Because the memcpy size is SIZE_MAX the copy always faults at the first unmapped page, so the effect is a reliable per-connection crash rather than a controlled heap write. Applications that load or inspect certificates from files, configuration or an API via tls_certificate_parse() crash on a single crafted blob.",
              "technical_analysis": "_private_asn1_parse() recurses through the DER tree tracking fields[] (the child index at each level). A primitive BIT STRING that is the third child (idx == 3) of the second nesting level, whose parent is the first child of the outer SEQUENCE (fields[0] == 1), is handed to tls_certificate_set_sign_key() at tlse.c:11590. That helper (tlse.c:4597-4605) strips a leading zero byte without checking that content remains, so a zero-length BIT STRING makes len become -1. tls_certificate_set_copy() (tlse.c:4546-4558) only rejects len == 0, so TLS_MALLOC(len + 1) becomes malloc(0) and memcpy(dst, src, (size_t)-1) is executed, which always faults.\\n\\nReachability: tls_consume_stream() -> tls_parse_message() -> _private_tls_consume_handshake() -> tls_parse_payload() -> tls_parse_certificate() -> asn1_parse() -> _private_asn1_parse(), i.e. the certificate comes straight from the wire. The same expression is reachable with no handshake at all through the exported tls_certificate_parse() API.\\n\\nThe 0x00 byte checked by val[0] is the tag byte of the TLV that follows the zero-length BIT STRING, so the trigger is fully attacker-controlled.",
              "remediation_steps": "Perform the leading-zero strip in tls_certificate_set_sign_key() only when the element actually has content, and make tls_certificate_set_copy() treat every non-positive length as \"no value\" while clearing the destination pointer. Note that fixing only the copy helper is insufficient: the pointer is already advanced past the value, so the length guard in the caller is required as well. Add DER fuzz coverage for zero-length primitive elements to prevent regressions.",
              "counterevidence": "A bare Certificate record without a preceding ServerHello does not crash (tested on TLS 1.2/1.3 client and server contexts) because tls_parse_payload() rejects it on handshake state - the attacker supplies the ServerHello trivially as the peer. Not an instrumentation artefact: the uninstrumented glibc build aborts with a malloc assertion on the 11-byte DER and SIGSEGVs on the 620-byte DER and the handshake flight. The parser does not simply reject malformed input - 154 legitimate certificates parse cleanly on the same build, and the trigger satisfies every length check before the vulnerable branch. No downstream control stops the negative length reaching memcpy.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating the huge memcpy can be steered into attacker-controlled mapped memory (turning the crash into a controlled write) would raise this to critical; conversely, proving no deployed TLSe build reaches tls_parse_certificate() or tls_certificate_parse() with peer-supplied DER would lower it.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: patched source rebuilt (ASAN and uninstrumented). tls_certificate_set_sign_key() now strips a leading zero only when len > 0, so a zero-length BIT STRING keeps len == 0; tls_certificate_set_copy() treats any len <= 0 as no value. Results on the patched build: 11-byte DER exit 0 (was 134), 620-byte DER exit 0 (was 139), 105-byte handshake flight exit 0 (was 139); no ASAN error for any of the three. An unrelated unfixed defect (the DTLS fragment overflow) still aborts on the same binary, so detection is not silently disabled.\\n\\nGate 2 bypass review: sibling call sites sharing the helper checked - tls_certificate_set_key() guards the decrement with len % 2, tls_certificate_set_priv() never decrements, tls_certificate_set_copy_date() requires len > 4. Alternate inputs tried: the 620-byte mutated DER, an ECDSA certificate with the same zero-length BIT STRING shape, and the same DER inside a handshake flight. All clean. The *member = NULL change also removes a latent use-after-free (a negative length previously skipped both branches and left a freed pointer in *member).\\n\\nGate 3 preserved behavior: the 154-certificate corpus parses without error on the patched build, identical to unpatched, and full handshakes still complete for TLS 1.0/1.2/1.3 with RSA and ECDSA certificates and for DTLS 1.2 (ECDHE-RSA-AES128GCM-SHA256, TLS-AES-128-GCM-SHA256, ECDHE-RSA-AES128CBC-SHA, ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128GCM-SHA256). Private-key loading still works - the handshake cannot complete otherwise.\\n\\nGate 4 repository checks: the library has no test suite or linter; the equivalent check is a clean gcc -O1 -g -fsanitize=address compile of tlse.c with no warnings from the changed lines, plus the corpus regression above.\\n\\nNote on line endings: this repository stores tlse.c with CRLF line terminators, so the suggestion blocks above use CRLF to match the file byte for byte.\\n\\nGap: no automated unit test exists to lock this in, so the regression was executed manually.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Write the 11-byte DER blob 3008050005000300000000 to a file; it is a SEQUENCE whose first element is a SEQUENCE containing three elements, the third a zero-length BIT STRING followed by a 0x00 byte.\\n2. Feed it to tls_certificate_parse() for the minimal local reproduction.\\n3. For the remote reproduction, wrap the DER in a Certificate handshake message: certificate_list length 0x00000e, certificate length 0x00000b, then the DER, wrapped in handshake type 0x0b inside a TLS record of type 0x16.\\n4. Prepend a ServerHello record so the handshake state machine accepts the Certificate, then feed the 105-byte stream to a TLS client context through tls_consume_stream().\\n5. Both cases abort: the instrumented build reports AddressSanitizer negative-size-param (size=-1) in memcpy; the uninstrumented build aborts in glibc malloc or dies with SIGSEGV.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-121",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stack buffer overflow in tls_stun_parse() key construction (WebRTC/STUN, pre-auth)\n\ntls_stun_parse() builds a \"username:realm:password\" string in a 1230-byte stack buffer without bounding the attribute lengths it has already accepted. A STUN binding request whose USERNAME (up to 513 bytes) and REALM (up to 763 bytes) attributes are both maximal overflows the buffer by up to 47 bytes with fully attacker-controlled data. The write happens before MESSAGE-INTEGRITY is checked, so an unauthenticated single UDP datagram triggers it."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 13201,
                  "endLine": 13214
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Root cause: unbounded concatenation into key[0x4CE], plus wrong pointer advance for REALM"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 13201,
                        "endLine": 13214
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {\r\n                    if ((unsigned int)(username_len + realm_len + pwd_len + 3) > sizeof(key))\r\n                        return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;\r\n                    ptr = key;\r\n                    memcpy(ptr, username, username_len);\r\n                    ptr += username_len;\r\n\r\n                    *ptr = ':';\r\n                    ptr ++;\r\n\r\n                    if ((realm) && (realm_len > 0)) {\r\n                        memcpy(ptr, realm, realm_len);\r\n                        ptr += realm_len;\r\n                        *ptr = ':';\r\n                        ptr ++;\r\n                    }"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Compute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm + password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer.",
                "markdown": "Compute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm + password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "3ee2eae78eb29db45855ee3ec8c5b3a97a61d71e55f65fbf2fef987eb26576a0"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.2",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "5605a5d295d260bd6a32c0a0207d8ec3904e1f071272a24aa660b89f6a38ac29",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.2,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:52:05 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - STUN / WebRTC DTLS path",
              "cwe": "CWE-121",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated attacker who can send a UDP datagram to a TLSe WebRTC/DTLS peer connection writes up to 47 bytes of fully controlled data past a 1230-byte stack buffer, corrupting the tls_stun_parse() frame. The overwritten region holds other locals of that frame, so the immediate demonstrated effect is corruption of program state in the TLS terminator plus an AddressSanitizer-detected abort; whether the overwrite reaches saved control data depends on the compiler's frame layout, and code execution was not demonstrated. Because the parser runs before any DTLS handshake or STUN authentication, no credential is required.",
              "technical_analysis": "tls_stun_parse() (tlse.c:13070) declares `char key[0x4CE]` at tlse.c:13085. Attribute handlers accept USERNAME up to 513 bytes (tlse.c:13184) and REALM up to 763 bytes (tlse.c:13233); nothing checks their sum against the destination, and the caller passes the library-generated 24-character local_pwd, so the maximum concatenation is 513 + 1 + 763 + 1 + 24 + 1 = 1303 bytes against a 1230-byte buffer.\\n\\nThe copies happen inside the MESSAGE-INTEGRITY handler (tlse.c:13201-13210) while assembling the HMAC key, i.e. before the integrity value is computed or compared, so no authentication is required. The same block also contains a separate defect: `ptr += username_len;` after copying the REALM advances by the wrong length, so the second separator and the password are written at incorrect offsets.\\n\\nEntry point: tls_peerconnection_iterate() at tlse.c:14006 calls tls_stun_parse() for any datagram that tls_is_stun() accepts, and tls_is_stun() only verifies the four magic-cookie bytes 0x2112A442. The parser is therefore reachable pre-authentication from one spoofable UDP datagram in any product using the WebRTC DTLS-SRTP support.",
              "remediation_steps": "Compute the total length of the concatenated key (username + realm + password + separators + terminator) and reject the message with TLS_BROKEN_PACKET when it cannot fit in the destination buffer, before performing any copy. Also correct the pointer advance for the REALM attribute, which currently uses the USERNAME length and therefore writes the separator and password at the wrong offsets. Alternatively, build the key with a length-tracking helper that cannot overrun, and lower the per-attribute limits so their sum is provably within the buffer.",
              "counterevidence": "Reachability is narrower than a plain TLS server: the only caller of tls_stun_parse() is tls_peerconnection_iterate(), so a deployment that never uses the WebRTC DTLS-SRTP peer connection cannot reach it - that is an exposure constraint, not a control, and it does not change the code defect. Within that path there is no control: tls_is_stun() only checks the magic cookie, and the copying happens before any authentication, so the attacker needs no credentials. I checked whether the destination buffer is protected some other way: it is a plain local array and gcc emits no stack protector for this function in the build tested, so nothing catches the overrun. On the uninstrumented build the process survived the write in the tested stack layout (the overrun lands in adjacent locals rather than in saved control data), which is why availability is scored low rather than high; the out-of-bounds write itself is unconditional and reproducible.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that the overwrite reaches the saved return address or a stack canary in a production build (giving control-flow hijack or a reliable crash) would raise this to critical; proof that the WebRTC/DTLS peer-connection feature is never compiled or deployed would lower it to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: the bounds check rejects any attribute combination whose concatenation cannot fit in key (username + realm + password + two separators + terminator), before any byte is copied; the only write path into key is guarded by it. Any request that would previously have overflowed now returns TLS_BROKEN_PACKET, so tls_stun_parse() and tls_peerconnection_iterate() return an error instead of corrupting the stack. Rebuilt and re-ran the 1332-byte PoC: no AddressSanitizer report, and the function returns an error.\\n\\nGate 2 bypass review: the second copy site (the password copy further down) is inside the same guarded block, so it cannot be reached with an unchecked length. Alternate input classes considered: repeated MESSAGE-INTEGRITY attributes (the first one fails the HMAC comparison and returns, so only one attempt per datagram), zero-length USERNAME/REALM (rejected by the same check), and non-4-byte-aligned attributes (the padding loop only advances the cursor; it does not affect the accepted lengths).\\n\\nGate 3 preserved behavior: legitimate 20-40 byte binding requests behave exactly as before; a request whose username+realm+password fits the buffer takes the same path and the same HMAC key derivation, with the ptr += realm_len correction making the derived key match the documented \"username:realm:password\" form instead of the previous wrong offset.\\n\\nGate 4 repository checks: no test suite or linter exists for this repository; the changed code was compiled cleanly with gcc -O1 -g under AddressSanitizer and the PoC was re-run.\\n\\nNote on line endings: this repository stores tlse.c with CRLF line terminators, so the suggestion block above uses CRLF to match the file byte for byte.\\n\\nGap: the password copy is bounded by the same new check rather than by a dedicated oversized-password test (the caller controls password length, not the attacker).",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build a STUN binding request: type 0x0001, attribute section length 0x0520, magic cookie 0x2112A442, a 12-byte transaction ID.\\n2. Append a USERNAME attribute (type 0x0006) with 513 bytes of filler, a REALM attribute (type 0x0014) with 763 bytes of filler, and a MESSAGE-INTEGRITY attribute (type 0x0008) with 20 zero bytes. The 513 and 763 limits are exactly the maxima the parser permits.\\n3. Deliver the 1332-byte datagram to tls_peerconnection_iterate() through a TLSRTCPeerConnection created with tls_peerconnection_context().\\n4. tls_is_stun() accepts it on the magic cookie alone; tls_stun_parse() builds the \"username:realm:password\" key and writes 47 bytes past char key[0x4CE].\\n5. The instrumented build reports AddressSanitizer stack-buffer-overflow (WRITE of size 763) at tlse.c:13210.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-287",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "TLS 1.3 client accepts Finished without CertificateVerify, enabling certificate impersonation\n\nThe TLS 1.3 client state machine does not require the server's CertificateVerify message. A server that performs its own key exchange, replays any certificate that satisfies the application's validation callback, and then sends Finished is accepted as authenticated - although it never proved possession of the certificate's private key. Any active man-in-the-middle can therefore impersonate the server to a TLSe client, including for certificates that validate against the configured trust roots."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 10078,
                  "endLine": 10079
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Require CertificateVerify before accepting the server Finished"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_parse_payload case 0x14 (Finished) / case 0x0F (CertificateVerify)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 10078,
                        "endLine": 10079
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "case 0x14:\n                DEBUG_PRINT0(\" => FINISHED\\n\");\n#ifdef WITH_TLS_13\n                /*  TLS 1.3 authenticates the peer with CertificateVerify; it is the\n                    only proof of possession of the certificate private key, and no\n                    PSK handshake exists in this implementation to omit it. */\n                if ((!context->is_server) && ((context->version == TLS_V13) || (context->version == DTLS_V13)) && (!context->hs_messages[8])) {\n                    DEBUG_PRINT0(\"* FINISHED WITHOUT CERTIFICATE VERIFY\\n\");\n                    payload_res = TLS_UNEXPECTED_MESSAGE;\n                    break;\n                }\n#endif"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Require the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS 1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of its certificate key.",
                "markdown": "Require the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS 1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of its certificate key."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "144f2c6cc82557f7be2419f34203bd4942946d8477e11e7bd6a9a7aaa3f93759"
