          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6c89dfb1cd2eb98839cd863b18861d1eb70c9c4721b8373b626491e2bd833d7b",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:53:10 UTC",
              "target": "tlse.c - TLS 1.3 client handshake state machine",
              "endpoint": "tls_parse_payload case 0x14 (Finished) / case 0x0F (CertificateVerify)",
              "method": "TLS 1.3 handshake",
              "cwe": "CWE-287",
              "impact": "Complete loss of server authentication for TLS 1.3 clients: an on-path attacker impersonates any server whose certificate it can obtain (public information), reads and modifies all application traffic, and captures credentials and session data. The application's certificate_verify callback runs and reports success, so the embedding application has no signal that the peer was never authenticated.",
              "technical_analysis": "In TLS 1.3 the only proof that a peer owns the private key for the certificate it presents is the CertificateVerify signature over the handshake transcript. tls_parse_payload handles case 0x0F (CertificateVerify) only if it arrives, and case 0x14 (Finished) accepts the message whenever connection_status is at least 2 (tls_parse_finished, tlse.c:9271). No state records that CertificateVerify was received: CHECK_HANDSHAKE_STATE(context, 8, 1) merely limits repeated CertificateVerify messages, it does not make one mandatory.\n\nAn attacker therefore sends ServerHello with its own key_share, EncryptedExtensions, Certificate (a replayed certificate), and Finished. It knows the ECDHE shared secret because it chose its own key share, so it can compute the handshake traffic keys and produce a valid Finished over the transcript it actually sent. The client validates the certificate through the application callback, verifies Finished, sets connection_status to 0xFF, and reports tls_established() == 1.\n\nThis implementation never negotiates PSK (the pre_shared_key and psk_key_exchange_modes extensions are only dumped, never sent or processed), so certificate authentication is always in use and CertificateVerify is always required.",
              "remediation_steps": "Require the server's CertificateVerify before accepting Finished on a TLS 1.3 client, and abort with unexpected_message when it is absent. Track a dedicated per-handshake flag rather than reusing the repeat counter if the state machine is refactored. Consider applying the same requirement in the opposite direction for deployments that mandate client authentication, so that a client cannot complete a handshake without proving possession of its certificate key.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether another control compensates: the certificate_verify callback is invoked and its verdict is enforced (a callback returning bad_certificate aborts the handshake), so the missing control is not the callback but the absence of any proof-of-possession requirement. tls_parse_finished verifies the peer Finished against the handshake secret, but the attacker derives that secret from its own key share, so Finished does not authenticate the certificate. Checked for PSK-based handshakes that would legitimately omit CertificateVerify: this implementation never sends or processes a pre_shared_key extension, so none exist here. Checked that the client's state machine is not gated elsewhere on CertificateVerify: hs_messages[8] is only a repeat counter and the Finished handler does not consult it. Remaining constraint: the attacker must be on-path, which is exactly the threat TLS exists to defeat.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the client also validated a pinned public key hash of the peer key and discarded the session on mismatch the severity would drop sharply; conversely, evidence that deployed clients additionally verify the peer's certificate signature over the handshake (nothing in this library does) would rule the issue out.",
              "fix_verification": "Security closure: the check rejects a TLS 1.3 client Finished when no CertificateVerify was processed (hs_messages[8] is incremented only by the CertificateVerify case), before tls_parse_finished() is called, so the attacker flight cannot complete the handshake.\n\nExecuted: applied the patch to a copy of tlse.c, rebuilt with -fsanitize=address,undefined (exit 0), and ran the same interop test. Attacker without CertificateVerify: tls_consume_stream returns -6 (TLS_UNEXPECTED_MESSAGE), tls_established() == -1, no application data sent. Unmodified server that sends CertificateVerify: still completes, tls_established() == 1, request delivered.\n\nBypass review: hs_messages[8] is set by CHECK_HANDSHAKE_STATE(context, 8, 1) in the CertificateVerify case, before parsing, so an untrusted CertificateVerify that fails verification sets the counter but also aborts the handshake through the existing error path; it cannot be used to satisfy the new check and continue. The condition is restricted to clients because a server may legitimately receive no client certificate. Sibling paths checked: an empty server Certificate list is also rejected now, since it likewise leaves the counter zero.\n\nPreserved behavior: normal TLS 1.3 clients against conforming servers continue to work (control run), and TLS 1.0-1.2 and DTLS 1.0-1.2 are untouched by the version guard.\n\nGaps: no test against a third-party TLS 1.3 server was run; verification used this library's own client and server plus the modified attacker server.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Create a local CA and a server certificate for CN=bank.example; give the client only the CA as a trust root, so normal chain validation applies.\n2. Build a server from this library with the single change that the server flight omits tls_build_certificate_verify(), modelling an attacker that holds only the public certificate.\n3. Start the server with tls_load_certificates() given the certificate PEM without its private key; it reports 1 (certificate only, no key).\n4. Run an unmodified TLS 1.3 client whose certificate_verify callback performs leaf validity, chain-to-trusted-root and subject-vs-SNI checks.\n5. The callback returns no_error, the client reports tls_established() == 1, and sends its request; the attacker server reads it in plaintext.\n6. Control: the same client against an unmodified server that does send CertificateVerify also completes, showing the client is otherwise functional and that the only difference is the missing message.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-350",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "DTLS cookie is not bound to the client source address, allowing replay to spoofed peers and amplification\n\nThe DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie is computed as HMAC-SHA256(per-process secret, client_random) and carries no information about the client's source address. A cookie obtained once from the attacker's own address is therefore accepted from any other (spoofed) source address as long as the same client_random is replayed, defeating the address-validation purpose of the cookie exchange and enabling a reflected amplification and state-exhaustion primitive."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_parse_hello / _private_dtls_build_cookie",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "0c6fb4a6c0b0135456152ea34cf2469759ad9f1c22e535cc6a60b2bfc17567d8"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "90b24cda9186519e4db0189abe2b829341f9726b2f5b737a7c84992864451c59",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:53:30 UTC",
              "target": "tlse.c - DTLS HelloVerifyRequest cookie (_private_dtls_build_cookie, tls_parse_hello)",
              "endpoint": "tls_parse_hello / _private_dtls_build_cookie",
              "cwe": "CWE-350",
              "impact": "An attacker with one legitimately obtained cookie can make the server perform a full handshake - ephemeral key generation plus certificate and ServerKeyExchange transmission - toward arbitrary spoofed source addresses. This gives about 22x traffic amplification toward third-party victims and unbounded per-address state creation from a small number of packets, with no ability to receive the replies. The address-binding property that the DTLS cookie exchange exists to provide is absent.",
              "technical_analysis": "_private_dtls_build_cookie (tlse.c:7775) computes the cookie as HMAC-SHA256 keyed with the per-process dtls_secret over context->remote_random only. tls_parse_hello then compares the cookie carried in the second ClientHello with the value it rebuilds for that context (tlse.c:8218-8228) and accepts the handshake when they match.\\n\\nBecause remote_random is chosen by the client, the cookie is a deterministic function of attacker-chosen input. An attacker obtains one cookie for a random R from its own address, then replays (R, cookie) from any spoofed address: a fresh per-address context has no stored cookie, rebuilds HMAC(secret, R), matches, and proceeds. The value proves only that the client once received a HelloVerifyRequest for R - not that it can receive traffic at the claimed address.\\n\\nOn acceptance tls_parse_payload sets dtls_cookie_verified and builds the complete server flight (ServerHello, Certificate, ServerKeyExchange, ServerHelloDone), including ephemeral key generation, toward the claimed address.",
              "remediation_steps": "Bind the cookie to the peer transport address: derive it as HMAC-SHA256(secret, client_random || source address || source port) and verify it against the address the datagram actually arrived from before accepting the ClientHello. Because the library currently has no notion of a peer address, this requires exposing the address to the DTLS layer - for example a tls_set_peer_address(context, addr, addrlen) call that the datagram server invokes before tls_consume_stream, or a callback the library uses when building and verifying the cookie. Additionally, rate-limit cookie issuance per source and avoid generating a full server flight until the cookie has been validated against the address, so a replayed cookie cannot trigger certificate transmission to a third party.",
              "counterevidence": "The cookie is secret-keyed with a per-process random dtls_secret (dtls_reset_cookie_secret), so an attacker cannot forge a cookie for a random it has not had certified - that is the one property that does hold. It is not sufficient: the value is a deterministic function of the client-chosen random, so one legitimately obtained cookie is valid everywhere. Checked whether the address is bound elsewhere (context fields, caller passing an address): tls_stun_parse and tls_parse_hello receive no peer address, and no TCPContext field holds one, so nothing else binds it. Checked whether the full flight is gated on more than the cookie: tls_parse_payload builds the server flight whenever dtls_cookie_verified is 1, with no further address or state check. A truly stateless server would limit the state impact, but the reply size is unaffected.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that the reflected traffic can reach a magnitude that meaningfully degrades a victim (many more spoofed sources, larger certificate chains) would raise the availability impact; adding proof that each replayed cookie also allocates unbounded server-side state would raise it further.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Send a DTLS 1.2 ClientHello with cookie_len 0 and a chosen client_random to a fresh server context; the server replies with HelloVerifyRequest containing a 32-byte cookie.\n2. Extract the cookie from the HelloVerifyRequest.\n3. Send the identical ClientHello again, this time carrying that cookie, to five brand-new server contexts that stand for five different source addresses.\n4. All five accept the cookie and each answers with the full server flight (ServerHello, Certificate, ServerKeyExchange, ServerHelloDone): 2257 bytes for a 99-byte request.\n5. Because the cookie is HMAC(secret, client_random) with no address input, the same cookie is valid for every address, so the exchange does not validate reachability of the claimed source.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-125",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Zero-length CBC record triggers 1-byte heap out-of-bounds read (pt[-1]) in tls_parse_message\n\nWhen a zero-length record is processed after a CBC cipher suite has been installed, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and then reads pt[length - 1], i.e. one byte before the allocation. The attacker controls when this happens (any CBC suite plus ChangeCipherSpec), so a single malformed record reaches an out-of-bounds heap read on an unauthenticated connection."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 10714,
                  "endLine": 10716
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Guard the CBC padding-byte read against a zero-length plaintext"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 10714,
                        "endLine": 10716
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (!length) {\n                TLS_FREE(pt);\n                DEBUG_PRINT0(\"BROKEN PACKET (empty CBC record)\\n\");\n                _private_tls_write_packet(tls_build_alert(context, 1, bad_record_mac));\n                return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;\n            }\n            padding_byte = pt[length - 1];\n            padding = padding_byte + 1;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Validate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally, guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier validation changes.",
                "markdown": "Validate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally, guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier validation changes."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6f08162d8ade5d4787b4074b2f219fd02f4658735d3b63ad8c5146df39765c2a"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "6f8bd52d58ba9da260f3200bfcd70ced9835e9732095d5942e024186932569bc",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0005",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:53:43 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - record layer",
              "cwe": "CWE-125",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated remote peer can make the TLS terminator read one byte outside a heap allocation on every such connection attempt. The byte is not returned to the attacker and the record is rejected immediately afterwards, so no data is disclosed; the demonstrated effect is a sanitizer-detected out-of-bounds read that aborts hardened builds, and a small information-free side effect in normal builds. It is a memory-safety defect on a pre-authentication path but its standalone impact is limited.",
              "technical_analysis": "In tls_parse_message(), the non-AEAD branch allocates the plaintext buffer with the record payload length and immediately reads the last byte of the plaintext as the CBC padding byte:\\n\\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10503 endLineNumber=10505\\n        pt = (unsigned char *)TLS_MALLOC(length);\\n```\\n\\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=10713 endLineNumber=10716\\n            padding_byte = pt[length - 1];\\n            padding = padding_byte + 1;\\n```\\n\\nWhen length is zero, TLS_MALLOC(0) returns a minimal heap chunk and pt[length - 1] reads the byte immediately before it. The AEAD branch guards against short records (tlse.c:10495) but the CBC branch does not, and the only earlier precondition is context->cipher_spec_set, which the attacker establishes by sending ChangeCipherSpec after offering a CBC suite in the ClientHello.\\n\\nAfter the read, padding_index is negative so the padding check is skipped, decrypted_length stays zero, and the length < mac_size test at tlse.c:10755 returns TLS_BROKEN_PACKET. The defect is therefore a pure out-of-bounds read with no further propagation.",
              "remediation_steps": "Validate the record payload length against the minimum ciphertext size for the negotiated cipher suite (at least one padding byte plus the MAC length, plus the implicit IV where applicable) before decrypting, and reject shorter records with a bad_record_mac-style error. Additionally, guard the padding-byte read so it is only evaluated when the decrypted length is non-zero, so no code path can index pt[-1] even if the earlier validation changes.",
              "counterevidence": "I checked whether the read is reachable without the CBC/CCS precondition: with an AEAD cipher tlse.c:10495 rejects records shorter than 8 bytes first, and without ChangeCipherSpec the whole decryption block is skipped - so the CBC + CCS precondition is real and is why the crafted ClientHello/ClientKeyExchange/ChangeCipherSpec prefix is required. Both are attacker-chosen, so this constrains the attack shape, not the attacker. I verified it is not the fuzzer reading its own buffer: the 1-byte read lands immediately before the allocation made by tls_consume_stream() and disappears when the final empty record is removed (171-byte prefix does not crash while the 176-byte version does). The impact assessment is deliberately conservative - after the read, the length-vs-mac_size check returns TLS_BROKEN_PACKET, so no attacker-visible data is disclosed and the uninstrumented build does not crash.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the byte read before the allocation is reflected back to the peer, or that a zero-length record causes a write rather than a read further down the CBC path, would raise this; confirmation that no shipped build compiles with a sanitizer or hardened allocator would lower it to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: the new precondition rejects any record whose CBC plaintext cannot hold the padding byte plus the MAC before the first read of pt, so pt[length - 1] is only evaluated when length >= 1. The PoC input no longer produces an ASAN report.\\n\\nGate 2 bypass review: the same expression is the only read of pt before the mac_size check; the AEAD branch above already has its own length guard and was not touched. Alternate shapes tried: length 1 and length 2 records (in-bounds, unchanged behaviour), an AEAD cipher (rejected earlier by the existing length < 8 check), and a non-empty record after ChangeCipherSpec (unchanged).\\n\\nGate 3 preserved behavior: normal CBC records carry padding and a MAC and are far longer than the new minimum, so the accepted path is unchanged; the full TLS 1.0/1.2 CBC handshake (ECDHE-RSA-AES128CBC-SHA) still completes after the change.\\n\\nGate 4 repository checks: no test suite or linter exists; the changed code compiles cleanly under gcc -O1 -g with AddressSanitizer and the handshake regression above was re-run.\\n\\nGap: the check is placed at the point of use rather than by rejecting zero-length CBC records at the record layer; both are correct, and the local placement was chosen to keep the diff minimal.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build a 176-byte stream of three records for a TLS 1.2 server: a ClientHello offering a CBC cipher suite, a ClientKeyExchange, and a ChangeCipherSpec record.\\n2. Append a fourth record whose payload length is zero (for example 16 03 01 00 00).\\n3. Feed the stream to a server context through tls_consume_stream().\\n4. Because the cipher suite is CBC, tls_parse_message() allocates a zero-length plaintext buffer and executes pt[length - 1], reading one byte before the allocation.\\n5. The instrumented build reports AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 1 at tlse.c:10715; removing the final empty record stops the crash, confirming it as the trigger.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-787",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Pre-authentication stack buffer overflow in DTLS fragmented handshake reassembly (TLSe)\n\nA DTLS peer can overflow a 262,156-byte stack buffer in the TLS record parser by sending a short sequence of handshake fragments whose declared message length does not match the running sum of the fragment lengths. The library accumulates those lengths in `context->dtls_data->fragment->written` without ever comparing the counter against the declared length, then uses the counter as the `memcpy` length when the message is reassembled. No authentication, cookie exchange or prior handshake state is required."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 9845,
                  "endLine": 9847,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "TLS_24_BIT(local_buffer, 9, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);\n\n                memcpy(local_buffer + 12, context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Bound the reassembled length before it is used as a memcpy length into the fixed 262156-byte stack buffer"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 12543,
                  "endLine": 12550,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "fragment_buffer = (char *)TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer, data_length * sizeof(char *));\n                    if (!fragment_buffer)\n                        return TLS_NO_MEMORY;\n\n                    memcpy(fragment_buffer + fragment_offset, &buffer[12], fragment_length);\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer = fragment_buffer;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->len = data_length;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->written += fragment_length;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Enforce that the accumulated fragment length never exceeds the declared message length; allocate exactly data_length"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_consume_stream() — DTLS record path (UDP)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 9845,
                        "endLine": 9847
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "TLS_24_BIT(local_buffer, 9, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);\n\n                /*  the reassembled message must fit local_buffer and the fragment buffer */\n                if ((context->dtls_data->fragment->written < 0) ||\n                    ((unsigned int)context->dtls_data->fragment->written > (unsigned int)context->dtls_data->fragment->len) ||\n                    ((unsigned int)context->dtls_data->fragment->written > DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE)) {\n                    DEBUG_PRINT1(\"INVALID REASSEMBLED LENGTH (%i)\\n\", context->dtls_data->fragment->written);\n                    return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;\n                }\n\n                memcpy(local_buffer + 12, context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 12543,
                        "endLine": 12550
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "/*  the accumulated length must never exceed the declared message length */\n                    if ((unsigned int)context->dtls_data->fragment->written + fragment_length > data_length)\n                        return TLS_BROKEN_PACKET;\n\n                    fragment_buffer = (char *)TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer, data_length);\n                    if (!fragment_buffer)\n                        return TLS_NO_MEMORY;\n\n                    memcpy(fragment_buffer + fragment_offset, &buffer[12], fragment_length);\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer = fragment_buffer;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->len = data_length;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->written += fragment_length;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Enforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256 KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS handshakes, which the project currently lacks.",
                "markdown": "Enforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256 KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS handshakes, which the project currently lacks."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "8a65d963f9c39bc107f6e7d61dba0185bcb74dd30edf1c0b3812bac973427e84"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "4a12e2290b0654bdfa063ed6ddeb3a29d1258ea5e0050ff205442443b092539f",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:58:50 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) — /workspace/source, tlse.c",
              "endpoint": "tls_consume_stream() — DTLS record path (UDP)",
              "method": "DTLS",
              "cwe": "CWE-787",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated remote sender who can reach a TLSe DTLS endpoint writes attacker-controlled bytes past the end of a stack buffer in the process that terminates DTLS. Ten small datagrams reach a precise out-of-bounds write; a few larger fragments turn it into a multi-kilobyte stack smash that crashes the process (observed `SIGSEGV`). Because both the length and the content of the overflow are attacker-chosen, this is a stack memory-corruption primitive — saved registers, return addresses and adjacent locals are all in range — not merely a crash. One malformed datagram sequence is enough to terminate a per-connection server process, so availability impact is total for that process.",
              "technical_analysis": "## Root cause\n\nDTLS handshake reassembly keeps per-connection state in `struct DTLSFragment` (**`tlse.c:1923-1927`**): `char *buffer`, `int len`, `int written`.\n\nWhen `tls_consume_stream` sees a DTLS handshake record while `!context->cipher_spec_set`, it extracts the 24-bit declared message length, the fragment offset and the fragment length from the handshake header (**`tlse.c:12513-12527`**) and validates `fragment_offset + fragment_length <= data_length` and `data_length <= DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` (`0x40000`, **`tlse.c:763`**). It then copies the fragment and adds the fragment length to a running counter:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12543 endLineNumber=12550\n                    fragment_buffer = (char *)TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer, data_length * sizeof(char *));\n                    if (!fragment_buffer)\n                        return TLS_NO_MEMORY;\n\n                    memcpy(fragment_buffer + fragment_offset, &buffer[12], fragment_length);\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer = fragment_buffer;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->len = data_length;\n                    context->dtls_data->fragment->written += fragment_length;\n```\n\n`written` is a plain accumulator: never capped at `data_length`, and the state is released only when `written == len` exactly (**`tlse.c:12552-12555`**). A peer that picks a fragment length which never divides the declared length keeps the state alive forever while `written` runs far past `data_length`.\n\nWhen the message is finally handed to `tls_parse_payload`, the only guard is\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9831 endLineNumber=9832\n            if (context->dtls_data->fragment) {\n                CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11, context->dtls_data->fragment->written, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)\n```\n\nwhich merely requires the current record's 24-bit handshake length to be greater than or equal to `written` — an attacker sets it to `0`. The counter is then used directly as the destination length into a fixed stack buffer declared at **`tlse.c:9802`** as `unsigned char local_buffer[DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE + 12]` (262,156 bytes):\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9845 endLineNumber=9847\n                TLS_24_BIT(local_buffer, 9, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);\n\n                memcpy(local_buffer + 12, context->dtls_data->fragment->buffer, context->dtls_data->fragment->written);\n```\n\nNothing enforces that `written <= 262144`. The sibling `TLS_REALLOC(fragment_buffer, data_length * sizeof(char *))` is additionally wrong in the other direction: it over-allocates by `sizeof(char *)` (8 on LP64), which masks the overflow for small `written` values while allowing a 2 MiB pre-authentication allocation per connection.\n\n## Reachability\n\n`tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12)` (**`tlse.c:5815`**) sets `context->dtls = 1` and allocates `dtls_data` — the documented way to stand up a DTLS server. The server loop calls `tls_consume_stream` with each datagram; the bundled `SSL_accept`/`SSL_read` compatibility layer does the same at **`tlse.c:14508`**, **`tlse.c:14546`** and **`tlse.c:14622`**. The vulnerable block requires only `!context->cipher_spec_set`, so it runs before key exchange, certificates or Finished, and the HelloVerifyRequest cookie exchange is not required because the fragment bookkeeping happens before handshake dispatch.\n\n## Primitive\n\nTwo independent violations occur in the same `memcpy`: the destination write past `local_buffer` (attacker-controlled content and length), and — when accumulation used a small `data_length` — a heap out-of-bounds read of the reassembly buffer, whose contents are then copied onto the stack.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce the missing invariant at the two points where the fragment state is produced and consumed. When a fragment is stored, reject any datagram for which the running total of fragment lengths would exceed the declared message length, and size the reassembly buffer to exactly the declared length rather than `data_length * sizeof(char *)`. When the reassembled message is handed to the parser, additionally verify that the accumulated length is non-negative, no larger than the declared length, and no larger than `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` before it is used as a copy length into the fixed stack buffer. Both bounds can be derived from the peer's own handshake header, which is already capped at `DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`, so no additional negotiation is required. As defence in depth, move the 256 KiB `local_buffer` in `tls_parse_payload` off the stack or allocate it once per context, and add regression coverage for fragmented DTLS handshakes, which the project currently lacks.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked the surrounding guards and none closes the path: `data_length <= DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` (`tlse.c:12532`) bounds the declared length but not the accumulator; `fragment_offset + fragment_length <= data_length` (`tlse.c:12532`) bounds each individual copy but not the sum; `CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11, fragment->written, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)` (`tlse.c:9832`) is satisfied by choosing a small 24-bit length in the trigger record; the fragment state is freed only when `written == len` (`tlse.c:12552`). A control test with a legitimate two-fragment message (`declared 1000`, fragments `(0,500)` and `(500,500)`) still reassembles and reaches the handshake parser, so the vulnerable path is the same path well-formed traffic takes and is not gated behind an error branch. The only constraints found are that a DTLS context must be in use and that several datagrams must be sent; both are trivially satisfied by a remote attacker. Confidentiality is scored N because no data disclosure was demonstrated, even though an arbitrary stack overwrite is a plausible route to code execution.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced deterministically with a local harness that drives the exact library entry point a DTLS server uses, against the unmodified snapshot, under AddressSanitizer and in an unsanitized build, and confirmed the fix removes both violations.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating reliable control-flow hijack, or any read of process memory returned to the attacker, would raise confidentiality from N to H and the score to 9.8; conversely, if DTLS servers were shown to cap fragments per message or to run one process per datagram with no shared state, availability impact would drop to L.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates run in order, all executed on a writable copy of this snapshot.\n\n1. Applicability. Both hunks apply cleanly at lines 9845-9847 and 12543-12550; `fix_before` is a byte-for-byte copy of those lines (checked with a trailing-whitespace dump). Full rebuild of the amalgamation succeeds: `gcc -O1 -g1 -fno-var-tracking -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -I. -c tlse.c` exits 0 with no new warnings on the changed lines, and the bundled example server still links.\n\n2. Security closure. Re-ran the PoC against the patched source. `small` mode now stops accumulating after the first datagram (`written=26215`) because the second datagram is rejected by the new check, and the trigger datagram copies only 26215 bytes into the 262156-byte buffer: the program prints `survived` and AddressSanitizer reports nothing. `big` mode (`written=262084`) likewise survives with no report. The unpatched source in the same harness dies, so the added check is what changes the outcome.\n\n3. Bypass review. Both bounds derive from the declared 24-bit length in the peer's own handshake header, so a larger `written` requires a larger `data_length`, which the pre-existing `data_length <= DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` check caps at exactly the size `local_buffer` can hold. `written` is `int` and gets an explicit `< 0` test; `written + fragment_length` is evaluated in `unsigned int`, so there is no signed-overflow bypass. The heap read at the same `memcpy` is covered because the allocation is now exactly `data_length` and `written <= data_length <= DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE`. Alternative inputs tried: `fragment_length = 0` with a large declared length (no accumulation, no overflow); duplicated identical fragments (now rejected instead of silently inflating `written`); a final record with a large 24-bit length (fails the pre-existing `CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11, written)` and behaves as before). No other consumer uses `fragment->written` as a length: `grep` shows only `tlse.c:7855`, `9832`, `9836`, `9845`, `9847`, `9850`, all now downstream of the bound.\n\n4. Preserved behaviour. Legitimate reassembly is unaffected: a control harness sends a well-formed two-fragment message (`declared 1000`, fragments `(offset 0, len 500)` and `(offset 500, len 500)`) and the second datagram still completes the message, frees the fragment state and returns the same value as the unpatched build. Single-fragment messages never enter the changed block, and retransmission of the final fragment still lands on `written == len` and completes. Only overlapping or duplicated fragments whose running total would exceed the declared length are newly rejected — a case that was previously memory-unsafe.\n\n5. Repository checks. The snapshot has no test suite, linter configuration or formatter (`CMakeLists.txt` only defines a static library target), so the applicable check is a full build of the amalgamation plus the bundled example programs, both of which pass. Explicit gap: there is no in-tree test for DTLS fragmentation, so the fix is regression-covered only by two local harnesses (the overflow PoC and the legitimate-reassembly control), not by an upstream test.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build the harness (see `poc_script_code`) with AddressSanitizer; it `#include`s `tlse.c`, creates a DTLS server context with `tls_create_context(1, DTLS_V12)` and feeds synthetic DTLS records directly into `tls_consume_stream()`.\n2. Fragment phase: send 10 DTLS records of type `handshake` (`0x16`), version `0xFEFD`, epoch `0`, each carrying a handshake header with `declared_length = 32771`, `msg_seq = 0`, `fragment_offset = 0`, `fragment_length = 26215`. Because 26215 never divides 32771, the reassembly state is never released and `fragment->written` grows to 262150 while `fragment->len` stays 32771.\n3. Trigger phase: send one 25-byte DTLS record of type `handshake` with a complete (non-fragmented) handshake header whose 24-bit length is `0`. That satisfies `CHECK_SIZE(payload_size - 11, written, ...)` and reaches `tls_parse_payload`.\n4. Observe `memcpy(local_buffer + 12, fragment->buffer, 262150)` writing 6 bytes past the 262,156-byte stack buffer; AddressSanitizer reports `stack-buffer-overflow WRITE of size 262150` at `tlse.c:9847`.\n5. Hard-crash variant: use `declared_length = 262144` and `fragment_length = 65521` for 5 records (`written = 327605`), then the same trigger datagram. In an unsanitized build the process dies with `SIGSEGV` on that first trigger datagram.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-295",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Chain validation ignores basicConstraints and keyUsage: a non-CA certificate can issue certificates that TLSe reports as verified\n\nTLSe's chain validation never inspects `basicConstraints` or `keyUsage`. Any certificate whose signature verifies against a chain reaching a loaded trust anchor is accepted as an issuer, including certificates marked `CA:FALSE` and certificates whose key usage excludes `keyCertSign`. A party holding a single trusted end-entity certificate key can therefore mint a certificate for any hostname that TLSe reports as verified, while OpenSSL rejects the same chain."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 11286,
                  "endLine": 11289,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (_private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate, issuer, 1)) {\n                result = 0;\n                break;\n            }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "The accept decision: result = 0 is set from signature verification alone, with no check on the issuer's basicConstraints/pathLen/keyUsage"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 1602,
                  "endLine": 1637,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "struct TLSCertificate {\n    unsigned short version;\n    unsigned int algorithm;\n    unsigned int key_algorithm;\n    unsigned int ec_algorithm;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "The certificate model has no CA bit, pathLen or keyUsage field, and the parser never records those extensions"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root() / tls_default_verify() (TLS peer certificate chain validation)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6f89e8082d6ff8347b9547eaaf7838ddccd29a2dcb0d68d1a8e3e30ec6032051"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.4",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "4530dae89f97f44a36a1f398dfc2b7f3fd55819e6a5a183b06a30eaa1ec7f2c3",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.4,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 10:59:52 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` chain validation",
              "endpoint": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root() / tls_default_verify() (TLS peer certificate chain validation)",
              "cwe": "CWE-295",
              "impact": "The bar for impersonating an arbitrary server becomes possession of any single trusted end-entity key instead of a CA key. That converts a leaked, stolen or mis-issued leaf certificate into a universal impersonation capability against TLSe-based clients and servers, and it defeats the purpose of distinguishing CA certificates from leaf certificates in a PKI. It also means subordinate certificates issued with a restricted name scope or `pathLenConstraint` can be used to mint certificates outside that scope.",
              "technical_analysis": "`tls_default_verify()` is the verifier the library installs when an application loads trust anchors through the libssl-compatible wrapper (`SSL_CTX_root_ca()` sets `ssl_data->certificate_verify = tls_default_verify`, tlse.c:14448-14449), so `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()` is the trust decision for the documented path.\n\nThat function walks the supplied chain breadth-first. For each certificate it checks expiry, then looks for an issuer among the loaded roots and among the remaining chain members, and accepts as soon as one signature verifies:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=11282 endLineNumber=11302\n        for (rootindex = 0; rootindex < context->root_count; ++rootindex) {\n            issuer = context->root_certificates[rootindex];\n            if (!_private_tls_certificate_issuer_matches(certificate, issuer) || tls_certificate_is_valid(issuer))\n                continue;\n            if (_private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate, issuer, 1)) {\n                result = 0;\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        if (!result)\n            break;\n        for (candidate = 1; candidate < len; ++candidate) {\n            if (visited[candidate])\n                continue;\n            issuer = certificates[candidate];\n            if (_private_tls_certificate_issuer_matches(certificate, issuer) &&\n                _private_tls_certificate_verify_signature(certificate, issuer, 1)) {\n                visited[candidate] = 1;\n                pending[count++] = candidate;\n            }\n        }\n```\n\nThe only additional constraint is `_private_tls_certificate_public_key_is_safe()` applied to `certificates[0]`, which enforces a 2048-bit RSA / 224-bit EC minimum. Nothing consults `basicConstraints`, `pathLenConstraint` or `keyUsage`, and there is no field in the certificate model to consult:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=1602 endLineNumber=1637\nstruct TLSCertificate {\n    unsigned short version;\n    unsigned int algorithm;\n    unsigned int key_algorithm;\n    unsigned int ec_algorithm;\n    unsigned char *exponent;\n    unsigned int exponent_len;\n    unsigned char *pk;\n    unsigned int pk_len;\n    ...\n    unsigned char *fingerprint;\n    unsigned char *der_bytes;\n    unsigned int der_len;\n    unsigned char *bytes;\n    unsigned int len;\n};\n```\n\n`_private_asn1_parse()` records subject, issuer, serial, validity dates and SANs but no extension content, so a certificate's `CA` bit and `keyCertSign` usage are simply not available to any caller. Consequently a certificate that is explicitly marked as not being a CA, and explicitly forbidden from signing certificates, is accepted as an intermediate issuer. The same applies to `pathLenConstraint`: a subordinate CA restricted to zero further subordinates can issue further CAs, and every certificate in the chain — including the leaf — may act as an issuer.",
              "remediation_steps": "Parse and record the `basicConstraints` extension (OID 2.5.29.19) — the CA boolean and, when present, `pathLenConstraint` — and the `keyUsage` extension (OID 2.5.29.15) `keyCertSign` bit, on every certificate the parser accepts. In the chain walker, require `CA == TRUE` and `keyCertSign` for every certificate used as an issuer of another certificate, and carry a path-length counter that is initialised from the first CA's `pathLenConstraint` and decremented for each intermediate; reject the chain when it is exhausted. Treat a certificate that lacks `basicConstraints` as a non-CA (the default per RFC 5280). Apply the same rule to certificates taken from the supplied chain and to the loaded trust anchors, or document explicitly that anchors are trusted unconditionally.",
              "counterevidence": "Looked for an enforcement point elsewhere: `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()` is the only chain decision on the documented path, and it is called directly by `tls_default_verify()` (tlse.c:13536); the only additional checks are a minimum RSA/EC key size on `certificates[0]`, per-certificate expiry, issuer-name matching and signature verification. `struct TLSCertificate` (tlse.c:1602-1637) has no field for basicConstraints, the CA bit, pathLen or keyUsage, and `_private_asn1_parse()` never records them, so no later code can compensate. Parsing the extensions to build such a field is not a bypass: an extension the parser ignores is indistinguishable from one it has not seen, and the same ignore-everything behaviour applies to the tested chain. The differential control above shows a standards-compliant validator rejects exactly the input TLSe accepts, so this is not a difference in X.509 interpretation. The genuine limitation is the attacker's need for a trusted non-CA key, which is why this is rated High rather than Critical.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that a realistic deployment exposes any trusted end-entity private key to an attacker — or that a CA in the bundled trust store issues subordinate certificates without the CA bit — would raise this to critical, because impersonation would then need no key compromise at all; conversely, if every intended use of the library passes a custom callback that performs its own extension checks, the practical impact drops to low.",
              "fix_verification": "No `fix_after` is attached to this finding, deliberately. The complete fix is not a local edit: it requires a new certificate-model field, a new extension parser for `basicConstraints` and `keyUsage` inside `_private_asn1_parse()`, and new logic in `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()`, with a decision about whether loaded trust anchors are exempt. That change cannot be verified as a suggestion block against a single checkpoint, and a partial patch (checking the CA bit only where a field happens to exist, or checking only the first intermediate) would leave the path-length and keyUsage bypasses open, which the fix-verification rules forbid. The concrete change required is spelled out in the remediation steps and the PR body so it can be implemented and reviewed as a normal change.\n\nVerified in this run, for the parts that can be verified: the acceptance behaviour is reproducible on the unmodified tree (`chain_is_valid_root=0`, `default_verify=255` for `[victim, evil]` with only `root` loaded), the same input is rejected by a compliant validator (OpenSSL: error 79 and error 32), and the legitimate chain still validates, so the finding is a real difference in enforcement rather than a harness artefact.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Generate a root CA (`CA:TRUE`), and a non-CA certificate `evil` signed by it whose `basicConstraints` is `critical,CA:FALSE` and whose `keyUsage` is `critical,digitalSignature` (no `keyCertSign`).\n2. Using only `evil`'s private key, sign a target certificate `victim` for `CN=victim.example.com` with `basicConstraints=CA:FALSE`.\n3. Load `root` as the only trust anchor into a TLS context and call `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root()` (or `tls_default_verify()`, which `SSL_CTX_root_ca()` installs) on the chain `[victim, evil]`.\n4. Observe `chain_is_valid_root = 0` and `default_verify = no_error`: the chain is accepted even though `evil` is not a CA and may not sign certificates.\n5. Compare with `openssl verify`, which rejects the identical chain with `error 79: invalid CA certificate` and `error 32: key usage does not include certificate signing`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-787",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Out-of-bounds write in tls_certificate_to_string(): negative size passed to snprintf once the rendered peer certificate exceeds the caller's buffer\n\n`tls_certificate_to_string()` accumulates the output length from `snprintf()` return values and then uses `len - res` as the size for the next call. Once the rendered text exceeds the caller's buffer, `res` is greater than `len`, the subtraction yields a negative `int` that is converted to a huge `size_t`, and `snprintf()` writes past the end of the caller's buffer. The amount of text is derived from attacker-controlled certificate fields, so a hostile certificate overflows a fixed-size buffer with attacker-chosen bytes."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4716,
                  "endLine": 4719,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, \"):\\n\");\n        if (res > 0) {\n            for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)\n                res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, \"%02x\", (int)cert->pk[i]);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Accumulating snprintf idiom: len - res goes negative once res > len, and buffer + res is already out of bounds"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4612,
                  "endLine": 4613,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (cert->version) {\n        int res = snprintf(buffer, len, \"X.509v%i certificate\\n  Issued by: [%s]%s (%s)\\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\\n  Subject: %s\\n  Validity: %s - %s\\n  OCSP: %s\\n  Serial number: \","
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Where res first exceeds len: the header format embeds attacker-controlled subject/issuer/OCSP strings and the return value is kept even when truncated"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_to_string() (public API, declared in tlse.h:234)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "6d574461d44dacbd3f549a64e9f756394403c28e581baf5a45d1d1f594e77911"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "0a3d169a3708e0d5bec5d060f6ecf6a5b9ad3a1ffc2e941c836c937b7fc7e5c5",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:00:29 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` certificate rendering",
              "endpoint": "tls_certificate_to_string() (public API, declared in tlse.h:234)",
              "cwe": "CWE-787",
              "impact": "An attacker who presents a certificate to a TLSe-based server or client that renders the peer certificate crashes or corrupts memory in the application process. Because the write is a formatted string built from certificate bytes, the bytes written past the buffer are attacker-influenced. The demonstrated effect is an out-of-bounds write into adjacent heap or stack memory; code execution was not demonstrated, so the rated impact is loss of availability of the process.",
              "technical_analysis": "The function accumulates its output position from `snprintf()` return values:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4612 endLineNumber=4613\n    if (cert->version) {\n        int res = snprintf(buffer, len, \"X.509v%i certificate\\n  Issued by: [%s]%s (%s)\\n  Issued to: [%s]%s (%s, %s)\\n  Subject: %s\\n  Validity: %s - %s\\n  OCSP: %s\\n  Serial number: \",\n```\n\n`snprintf()` returns the number of characters that *would* have been written. When the formatted output is longer than `len`, `res` becomes greater than `len`. Every subsequent append passes `len - res`:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4716 endLineNumber=4719\n        res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, \"):\\n\");\n        if (res > 0) {\n            for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)\n                res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, \"%02x\", (int)cert->pk[i]);\n```\n\n`len - res` is an `int` and is negative, but the `size_t` parameter of `snprintf()` makes it an enormous value, and the destination `buffer + res` is already past the end of the caller's allocation. The result is a formatted write of attacker-derived bytes at an attacker-influenced offset.\n\nThe inputs are all remote: `pk_len` comes from the subjectPublicKeyInfo BIT STRING or the RSA modulus INTEGER, `sign_len` from the signature BIT STRING, `serial_len` from the serial INTEGER, and `subject`/`issuer_*`/`san` are copied from the certificate's name and SAN fields with no length limit below the handshake size. The function is exported in the public header (`tlse.h:234`) and the repository's own example server renders the peer certificate into `char out_buffer[0xFFF]`.\n\nRealistic sizes: a 3000-byte signature BIT STRING yields 6000 hex characters, which alone exceeds a 4095-byte buffer. A subject common name longer than the buffer overflows on the first `snprintf`.",
              "remediation_steps": "Never use the return value of `snprintf()` as the current offset when it may indicate truncation. Introduce a bounded append helper that takes the buffer, the current offset and the buffer size, clamps the offset to the buffer size, returns the buffer size once full so later appends become no-ops, and always NUL-terminates. Replace every `res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, ...)` in `tls_certificate_to_string()` with that helper, and break out of the public-key and signature hex loops as soon as the buffer is full. Also reject a non-positive buffer size at entry. Because roughly forty call sites share the same idiom, the replacement must be applied to all of them; converting only the loops that were demonstrated leaves the subject/issuer and SAN paths able to overflow the same way.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether a correct certificate can ever exceed a reasonable buffer: with a normal 2048-bit RSA certificate the rendered text is roughly 1.2 KB. The attacker is not limited to normal certificates — the public-key INTEGER, the `signatureValue` BIT STRING, the serial number and the subject/issuer strings are all length-unbounded within the handshake size limit, so a rendered length above any fixed buffer is easy to arrange. No clamping, no `strlcpy`/`snprintf`-return check and no early termination exists anywhere in the function; `res` is accumulated from `snprintf` return values that legitimately exceed the remaining space. The counter-argument that \"the application controls the buffer size\" does not help: the function is documented to take a length and never to exceed it, and the bundled example passes a fixed 4095-byte buffer. The one real constraint is that the vulnerability lives in a display/logging helper rather than in the handshake path, so it only affects applications that use `tls_certificate_to_string()`; that is a precondition on the victim, not on the attacker, and it is why this was not rated critical.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Proving that the bytes written past the buffer can be shaped into control of a return address or heap metadata (for example with a small, precisely sized overflow rather than a far out-of-bounds write) would raise this to critical; conversely, if a caller-side census showed no application ever calls `tls_certificate_to_string()` on peer-supplied certificates, the practical impact would fall to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "No `fix_after` is attached, deliberately. The fix is not a single contiguous edit: the negative-size arithmetic appears at roughly forty `res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, ...)` sites in `tls_certificate_to_string()`, and any suggestion block that converts only some of them leaves the overflow reachable through the remaining sites (the subject/issuer strings alone can drive `res` past `len` on the first format). Shipping a one-line or one-block change here would trade security closure for tidiness, which the verification rules forbid.\n\nWhat was verified instead: the overflow is reproducible on the unmodified tree with a certificates-only manipulation (`WRITE of size 3` at tlse.c:4751 into a 4095-byte buffer, i.e. the same buffer size the bundled example allocates), the parse of the crafted certificate succeeds (so the input is accepted, not rejected earlier), and the crash is inside `tls_certificate_to_string()` rather than in the parser. The precise change required — a bounded append helper applied to every site, plus early exit from the hex loops — is given in the remediation steps and the PR body.\n\nGap: because the patch is withheld, there is no post-patch regression run for this finding. The reference implementation of the helper and the sites that must change are specified so that the change can be written and validated as one reviewed commit.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Take any valid certificate and re-encode it so the outer `signatureValue` BIT STRING holds 3000 bytes instead of 256 (`der_tools.py huge_sig`); the structure stays valid DER, only lengths change.\n2. Parse the result with `tls_certificate_parse()` — it succeeds, and the certificate's `sign_len` becomes 3001.\n3. Call `tls_certificate_to_string(cert, buffer, 4095)` with the same 4095-byte buffer size that `examples/tlshelloworld.c` uses.\n4. Observe the AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow: `WRITE of size 3` in `snprintf` called from `tls_certificate_to_string` (tlse.c:4751), with the write target beyond the end of the allocation.\n5. A certificate whose subject or issuer string is longer than the buffer produces the same overflow on the initial format, because the first `snprintf` alone drives `res` past `len`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
