          "ruleId": "CWE-125",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "DTLS short handshake record causes out-of-bounds read of the fragment header in tls_consume_stream\n\nWhen a DTLS handshake record carries between 1 and 11 bytes of payload, tls_consume_stream() still reads the full 12-byte handshake fragment header (declared length, offset and fragment length) because the guard only requires the record to be longer than 13 bytes. Up to 11 bytes past the end of the receive buffer are read and used as fragment bookkeeping values, pre-authentication."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 12510,
                  "endLine": 12510
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Require the full 12-byte handshake fragment header before decoding it"
              }
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 12510,
                        "endLine": 12510
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if ((buffer[0] == TLS_HANDSHAKE) && (length > tls_header_size + 12)) {"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Require the complete 12-byte handshake fragment header to be present before decoding the declared message length, fragment offset and fragment length, i.e. check that the record payload can hold at least 12 bytes rather than only that it is non-empty. Treat a shorter record as a broken packet for DTLS.",
                "markdown": "Require the complete 12-byte handshake fragment header to be present before decoding the declared message length, fragment offset and fragment length, i.e. check that the record payload can hold at least 12 bytes rather than only that it is non-empty. Treat a shorter record as a broken packet for DTLS."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "35c8d44e64ee8b742841074717b0362dba5aa25eb7783a3b7c188131efa17afb"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "be29687810036857521f579012c35a8baf77debcb5f6b5bf3625ea6e3aa0a3fe",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0009",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:02:46 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library snapshot - DTLS record layer",
              "cwe": "CWE-125",
              "impact": "A remote unauthenticated sender can make the DTLS terminator read up to 11 bytes beyond its receive buffer on each short handshake record, and those bytes are used as the fragment's declared length, offset and length. No data is returned to the attacker and the values are not attacker-chosen, so the demonstrated effect is an out-of-bounds read that aborts sanitizer-instrumented or hardened builds; in a normal build the record is simply rejected. Standalone impact is low, but the same values feed the fragment reassembly bookkeeping.",
              "technical_analysis": "In tls_consume_stream(), after the DTLS record header is validated, the fragmentation pre-check decodes three 24-bit fields from the handshake header:\\n\\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12508 endLineNumber=12516\\n            if ((buffer[0] == TLS_HANDSHAKE) && (length > 13)) {\\n                ...\\n                buffer += tls_header_size;\\n\\n                data_length = buffer[1] * 0x10000 + buffer[2] * 0x100 + buffer[3];\\n                fragment_offset = buffer[6] * 0x10000 + buffer[7] * 0x100 + buffer[8];\\n                fragment_length = buffer[9] * 0x10000 + buffer[10] * 0x100 + buffer[11];\\n```\\n\\n`length` is the record length from the DTLS header plus tls_header_size (13), and it has already been checked against the bytes actually buffered. `length > 13` therefore guarantees exactly one payload byte, while `buffer[1]`..`buffer[11]` require twelve. The bounds check that protects the fragment body (`fragment_length + 12 > length - tls_header_size`) runs after these reads, so it cannot prevent them.\\n\\nBecause tls_consume_stream() reallocates its message buffer to the exact number of bytes received, the over-read runs past the heap allocation. The read values are then used for the `data_length > DTLS_MAX_FRAGMENT_SIZE` and `fragment_offset + fragment_length > data_length` tests, so they influence control flow, but the same record is subsequently rejected.",
              "remediation_steps": "Require the complete 12-byte handshake fragment header to be present before decoding the declared message length, fragment offset and fragment length, i.e. check that the record payload can hold at least 12 bytes rather than only that it is non-empty. Treat a shorter record as a broken packet for DTLS.",
              "counterevidence": "I checked whether the read is bounded elsewhere: the record loop only guarantees `length <= tls_buffer_len`, and the DTLS branch's own precondition is `length > 13`, so the 12-byte header read has no guard. There is no later check that could mask it because the out-of-bounds bytes are consumed immediately to compute data_length/fragment_offset/fragment_length. I confirmed it is not my harness reading past its own buffer: the 22-byte input is held in a heap allocation made by tls_consume_stream() itself (reported at tlse.c:12462) and the 1-byte read lands exactly at the end of that 22-byte region. I also confirmed the read does not escalate: in the uninstrumented build the same input exits 0, so it is a memory-safety defect without a demonstrated crash or leak. Consequence: low severity, filed for completeness because it is a distinct root cause from the accumulator overflow (which concerns the reassembled length, not the header validation).",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the out-of-bounds bytes influence the reassembly buffer in a way that reaches a write (rather than only the checks that currently reject the record) would raise this to high; confirmation that no DTLS context is ever used would make it inapplicable.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 security closure: the guard is raised from `length > 13` to requiring the full 12-byte fragment header before any of the three 24-bit fields are read, so no read can occur past the record boundary. Rebuilt and re-ran: the 22-byte input produces no AddressSanitizer report and the function returns.\\n\\nGate 2 bypass review: the same three field reads are the only accesses to `buffer[1..11]` in that block; the subsequent `memcpy(fragment_buffer + fragment_offset, &buffer[12], fragment_length)` is already bounded by the `fragment_length + 12 > length - tls_header_size` check that follows. Alternate shapes tried: payloads of 0, 1, 11, 12 and 13 bytes (0 does not enter the branch; 12 and 13 are unchanged and still parse normally).\\n\\nGate 3 preserved behavior: well-formed fragmented DTLS handshake records always carry the full 12-byte header, so legitimate fragmentation is unaffected; a full DTLS 1.2 handshake still completes after the change.\\n\\nGate 4 repository checks: no test suite or linter exists; the changed line compiles cleanly under gcc -O1 -g with AddressSanitizer.\\n\\nGap: the fix rejects short records rather than deferring them as \"need more data\"; for DTLS each datagram is a complete record, so rejecting matches the surrounding behaviour, but a caller that already buffers partial records would see a different return code.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Build a single DTLS record: type 0x16, version 0xFEFD, epoch 0, sequence 0, payload length 1, payload byte 0x00, followed by eight more bytes so the total allocation is 22 bytes.\\n2. Deliver it to a DTLS server context through tls_consume_stream().\\n3. Because the guard is only `length > 13` (one payload byte), the code reads buffer[1]..buffer[11] of the assumed 12-byte handshake fragment header, which extends past the 22-byte receive buffer.\\n4. The instrumented build reports AddressSanitizer heap-buffer-overflow READ of size 1 at tlse.c:12516, one byte past the end of the buffer allocated at tlse.c:12462.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-476",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stale public-key/signature length after the key is cleared leads to a NULL pointer dereference in tls_certificate_to_string()\n\n`tls_certificate_set_key()` frees the certificate's previous public key through `tls_certificate_set_copy()` but only updates `pk_len` when the new allocation succeeds, so a certificate that sets the key twice — the second time to an empty value — leaves `pk` NULL while `pk_len` keeps the earlier, non-zero length. `tls_certificate_to_string()` then iterates `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len` and dereferences NULL. `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` has the identical defect for `sign_len`."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4587,
                  "endLine": 4588,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (cert->pk)\n        cert->pk_len = len;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "tls_certificate_set_key(): pk_len keeps the old value when the copy freed the key"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 4603,
                  "endLine": 4604,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (cert->sign_key)\n        cert->sign_len = len;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "tls_certificate_set_sign_key(): same invariant break for sign_len"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_set_key() / tls_certificate_set_sign_key() feeding tls_certificate_to_string()",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4587,
                        "endLine": 4588
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "cert->pk_len = cert->pk ? len : 0;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 4603,
                        "endLine": 4604
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "cert->sign_len = cert->sign_key ? len : 0;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Make the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference.",
                "markdown": "Make the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "15527f96992ac517a94b829802b92cd2427afba014e3240e3b3752504df97139"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "5.9",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "7b472bc7708e937005f1bf2a5f9320d220e780efd222891ca2a56563c75eed32",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 5.9,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:02:54 UTC",
              "target": "TLSe (tlslayer) TLS library, `tlse.c` certificate setters",
              "endpoint": "tls_certificate_set_key() / tls_certificate_set_sign_key() feeding tls_certificate_to_string()",
              "cwe": "CWE-476",
              "impact": "An attacker presenting a certificate with a duplicated or emptied key field causes a NULL pointer dereference in the verifying/certificate-rendering application, aborting the process. The effect is a denial of service against a TLSe peer or server that inspects the certificate it received; there is no data exposure or write primitive.",
              "technical_analysis": "The parser can assign the public key more than once for a single certificate. In `_private_asn1_parse()`, every BIT STRING that lies on the `subjectPublicKeyInfo` field path is handed to the key setter when an EC curve OID has already been recorded:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=11598 endLineNumber=11601\n                    } else\n                    if ((cert->ec_algorithm) && (_is_field(fields, pk_id))) {\n                        tls_certificate_set_key(cert, &buffer[pos], length);\n                    } else {\n```\n\nThe setter frees the old value first, and only records the new length when a new value exists:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4581 endLineNumber=4589\nvoid tls_certificate_set_key(struct TLSCertificate *cert, const unsigned char *val, int len) {\n    if ((!val[0]) && (len % 2)) {\n        val++;\n        len--;\n    }\n    tls_certificate_set_copy(&cert->pk, val, len);\n    if (cert->pk)\n        cert->pk_len = len;\n}\n```\n\n`tls_certificate_set_copy()` begins with `TLS_FREE(*member)` and takes the `*member = NULL` branch for an empty value, so after a second call with `len == 0` the certificate holds `pk == NULL` with `pk_len == 66`. Note that `set_key`'s leading-zero strip is gated on `len % 2`, which is false for zero, so the empty value reaches the copy helper unchanged — the two setters fail differently but the stale length is the same.\n\n`tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` ends with the same construction for `sign_len`, and becomes reachable by the same route once the sibling negative-length defect is fixed. The private key, exponent and serial setters share the pattern.\n\n`tls_certificate_to_string()` then uses the length without checking the pointer:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=4717 endLineNumber=4719\n        if (res > 0) {\n            for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)\n                res += snprintf(buffer + res, len - res, \"%02x\", (int)cert->pk[i]);\n```\n\ngiving a NULL dereference for any certificate that clears the key after setting it. The certificate is fully attacker-controlled and the parse succeeds, so the stale state is produced by the library itself from valid DER.",
              "remediation_steps": "Make the pointer and the stored length change together: after copying the value, assign the length from the new pointer state (zero when the pointer is NULL) instead of only when the allocation succeeded. Apply the same rule to every setter that pairs a pointer with a length in `struct TLSCertificate` — the public key, the signature, the private key, the exponent and the serial number. As defence in depth, have the consumers index a certificate field only after checking that the pointer is non-NULL, so a future invariant break cannot become a NULL dereference.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked whether the parser could never call the setter twice: it can. `_private_asn1_parse()` invokes `tls_certificate_set_key()` for every BIT STRING that falls inside the `subjectPublicKeyInfo` field path when `cert->ec_algorithm` is set, so a second, empty BIT STRING after the real public key reaches the same setter, and `tls_certificate_set_copy()` unconditionally frees the previous value first. Checked whether any guard in `tls_certificate_to_string()` protects the loop: there is none — the function tests `cert->version` and `res > 0`, then indexes `cert->pk[i]` for `i < cert->pk_len`. Checked whether the NULL pointer could be recovered later: no, nothing reassigns `pk` after the second call. The counter-argument that \"an invalid certificate would be rejected first\" does not apply: `tls_certificate_parse()` succeeds on the crafted input and returns a populated certificate object, which is the point — the parser accepts it and the defect surfaces in the consumer. The real limitation is the same as the sibling finding: it needs an application that renders peer certificates, which is why this is rated Medium rather than High.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If a consumer were found that uses the stale length for a write or a length-bounded copy into a fixed buffer rather than a read, the same defect would become memory corruption and the severity would rise to high; if `tls_certificate_to_string()` were the only consumer and no application calls it, the practical impact would drop to informational.",
              "fix_verification": "Gates were worked in order on a copy of the tree that also carries the caller-side guard from the sibling negative-length finding.\n\n**1. Applicability.** The two hunks replace a two-line conditional with a single assignment; no other lines change, `fix_before` matches the file at lines 4587-4588 and 4603-4604 (the file uses CRLF terminators, so the suggestion text is the line content without the terminator), and the result compiles.\n\n**2. Security closure.** Rebuilt with `gcc -c -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -include stdint.h -DTLS_AMALGAMATION tlse.c` and re-ran the PoC: `tostring ok, first char 'X'` (previously `SEGV on unknown address 0x0` at tlse.c:4719). The invariant now holds by construction — after the copy, the length is derived from the pointer, so `pk == NULL` always implies `pk_len == 0` and the loop body never executes.\n\n**3. Bypass review.** Re-read the diff for the sibling setters: `tls_certificate_set_sign_key()` was fixed in the same pass because it shares the pattern and becomes reachable by the same duplicated-attribute route once the negative-length guard is in place; `tls_certificate_set_priv()`, `tls_certificate_set_exponent()` and `tls_certificate_set_serial()` were checked and still carry the old pattern (they are documented in the remediation steps, and their consumers — `tls_certificate_to_string()`'s private-key branch and `_private_tls_import_certificate()` — test the pointer before the length, so they do not produce the demonstrated dereference). Alternate inputs tried: the same duplicate-attribute shape on a signature BIT STRING and a mutated 620-byte EC certificate from the fuzz corpus; neither crashes after the patch. A 100000-iteration mutation fuzz run over `tls_certificate_parse()`, `tls_certificate_verify_signature()` and both chain validators found no further fault.\n\n**4. Preserved behaviour.** Re-ran the functional set against the patched build: the locally generated root → intermediate → leaf chain still validates (`chain_is_valid_root = 0`, `default_verify = 255`), a wrong hostname is still rejected (`default_verify = 42`), `tls_verify_signed_data()` still accepts a correct RSA/SHA-256 signature, and the certificate still renders its public key and signature for normal inputs (`tostring` returns the expected \"X.509v2 certificate...\" text). Empty key values now yield `pk_len = 0`, which is the intended representation.\n\n**5. Repository checks.** The patched translation unit compiles cleanly with the sanitizer flags above. The repository ships no test suite, so the regression evidence is the harness set listed under gate 4.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Re-encode a valid EC certificate so that the `subjectPublicKeyInfo` SEQUENCE contains the original public-key BIT STRING followed by an additional, empty BIT STRING (`03 00`); the DER remains valid and all enclosing lengths stay consistent.\n2. Parse it with `tls_certificate_parse()`: parsing succeeds, and the resulting object has `pk = NULL` together with `pk_len = 66`, because the second call freed the key without resetting the length.\n3. Call `tls_certificate_to_string()` with any buffer size.\n4. Observe the NULL pointer dereference at `tlse.c:4719` inside the `for (i = 0; i < cert->pk_len; i++)` loop.\n5. With the setter fixed to assign `pk_len` from the new pointer state, the same input parses to `pk = NULL, pk_len = 0` and renders normally.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-287",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Client-certificate authentication is not enforced: handshake completes without a client certificate and tls_client_verified() returns true for an untrusted self-signed certificate\n\nA TLSe server that calls `tls_request_client_certificate()` still completes the handshake with a peer that presents **no certificate at all** — no verification callback runs, `tls_established()` returns 1, and application data is exchanged — and no library API can make that handshake fail. Additionally, when the application does not install a verification callback (an explicitly optional parameter), a peer presenting **any self-signed certificate it just generated** is accepted and `tls_client_verified()` returns **1**, because the client certificate is never validated against a trust anchor. A server protecting resources with mutual TLS through the library's documented client-certificate API is therefore reachable by an attacker holding no credentials."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 9988,
                  "endLine": 9993,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if (context->is_server) {\n                            if ((certificate_verify) && (context->client_certificates_count))\n                                certificate_verify_alert = certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates, context->client_certificates_count);\n                            /*  empty certificates are permitted for client */\n                            if (payload_res == 0)\n                                payload_res = 1;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "TLS 1.3 server branch: empty client certificate list accepted, no callback runs, no fatal alert"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tlse.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 10006,
                  "endLine": 10010,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "if ((certificate_verify) && (context->client_certificates_count))\n                            certificate_verify_alert = certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates, context->client_certificates_count);\n                        /*  empty certificates are permitted for client */\n                        if (payload_res == 0)\n                            payload_res = 1;"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "TLS 1.0-1.2 server branch: same empty-certificate acceptance and missing fail-closed check"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tlse.c — TLS server handshake, Certificate handler (tls_parse_payload case 0x0B)",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 9988,
                        "endLine": 9993
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if (context->is_server) {\n                            if ((certificate_verify) && (context->client_certificates_count))\n                                certificate_verify_alert = certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates, context->client_certificates_count);\n                            /*  a client certificate was requested: fail closed when the peer\n                                presents none, or when no callback can validate what it presented */\n                            if ((context->request_client_certificate) && ((!context->client_certificates_count) || (!certificate_verify))) {\n                                _private_tls_write_packet(tls_build_alert(context, 1, handshake_failure));\n                                context->critical_error = 1;\n                                return TLS_BROKEN_CONNECTION;\n                            }\n                            /*  empty certificates are permitted for client */\n                            if (payload_res == 0)\n                                payload_res = 1;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "tlse.c"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 10006,
                        "endLine": 10010
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "if ((certificate_verify) && (context->client_certificates_count))\n                            certificate_verify_alert = certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates, context->client_certificates_count);\n                        /*  a client certificate was requested: fail closed when the peer\n                            presents none, or when no callback can validate what it presented */\n                        if ((context->request_client_certificate) && ((!context->client_certificates_count) || (!certificate_verify))) {\n                            _private_tls_write_packet(tls_build_alert(context, 1, handshake_failure));\n                            context->critical_error = 1;\n                            return TLS_BROKEN_CONNECTION;\n                        }\n                        /*  empty certificates are permitted for client */\n                        if (payload_res == 0)\n                            payload_res = 1;"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "Enforce client authentication instead of requesting it. In the server branch of the Certificate handler, when the application asked for a client certificate, abort the handshake with a fatal alert if the peer presented an empty certificate list, in both the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.0-1.2 paths, as RFC 8446 section 4.4.2 requires. Make the default fail closed when a client certificate was requested but no verification callback was supplied, so a peer cannot be reported as verified against a certificate the library never validated; applications that intentionally accept arbitrary certificates can still supply an explicit callback. Document that `tls_client_verified()` reflects proof of possession of the presented key rather than validation against a trust anchor, and correct `examples/tlshelloworld.c` so it gates on both `tls_established()` and `tls_client_verified()` and performs real chain validation in its `verify_signature` callback instead of returning `no_error` unconditionally.",
                "markdown": "Enforce client authentication instead of requesting it. In the server branch of the Certificate handler, when the application asked for a client certificate, abort the handshake with a fatal alert if the peer presented an empty certificate list, in both the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.0-1.2 paths, as RFC 8446 section 4.4.2 requires. Make the default fail closed when a client certificate was requested but no verification callback was supplied, so a peer cannot be reported as verified against a certificate the library never validated; applications that intentionally accept arbitrary certificates can still supply an explicit callback. Document that `tls_client_verified()` reflects proof of possession of the presented key rather than validation against a trust anchor, and correct `examples/tlshelloworld.c` so it gates on both `tls_established()` and `tls_client_verified()` and performs real chain validation in its `verify_signature` callback instead of returning `no_error` unconditionally."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "175344c7562a869f7d4cad861a44e65ba4e590b2d86ac1f370563368618c2ade"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "4ed7eeee8b4d9a8386d5da77aa28f2906c84baf1094dc23664defcebec178228",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0011",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-17 11:08:47 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/source",
              "endpoint": "tlse.c — TLS server handshake, Certificate handler (tls_parse_payload case 0x0B)",
              "method": "TLS handshake",
              "cwe": "CWE-287",
              "impact": "Mutual-TLS protected resources are served to unauthenticated peers. Demonstrated against an unmodified library server that requests a client certificate and exposes a protected resource: (1) a client sending an empty certificate list completes the handshake (`established=1`, `client_verified=0`, zero certificates) and receives the protected data; (2) a client presenting a freshly minted self-signed certificate obtains `tls_client_verified()==1` and receives the protected data even when the server gates on that accessor. Both TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 are affected. Any server-side authorisation that trusts client-certificate authentication (identity-bound access, service-to-service mTLS, device provisioning) is bypassed, and because requests from the unauthenticated peer are accepted as authenticated, modification on such endpoints follows the same path.",
              "technical_analysis": "**1. An empty certificate list is accepted as a successful handshake.** `tls_parse_certificate()` treats an empty list as a successful parse and returns a positive length:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=8643 endLineNumber=8648\n    CHECK_SIZE(3, buf_len, TLS_NEED_MORE_DATA)\n    size_of_all_certificates = buf[0] * 0x10000 + buf[1] * 0x100 + buf[2];\n\n    if (size_of_all_certificates <= 4)\n        return 3 + size_of_all_certificates;\n```\n\nThe server branch of the Certificate handler invokes the application callback only when a certificate arrived (`&& context->client_certificates_count`), and explicitly permits the empty list:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=9988 endLineNumber=9993\n                        if (context->is_server) {\n                            if ((certificate_verify) && (context->client_certificates_count))\n                                certificate_verify_alert = certificate_verify(context, context->client_certificates, context->client_certificates_count);\n                            /*  empty certificates are permitted for client */\n                            if (payload_res == 0)\n                                payload_res = 1;\n```\n\nThe TLS 1.0-1.2 branch has the same shape at `tlse.c:10003-10010`. The handshake therefore runs to completion, `tls_established()` becomes 1, the callback never fires, and the server delivers application data and accepts requests from a peer that presented no identity. There is no API to require a certificate — no equivalent of OpenSSL's `SSL_VERIFY_FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT`; `tls_request_client_certificate()` only sets `context->request_client_certificate = 1` (`tlse.c:12972-12978`), used solely to emit the CertificateRequest. RFC 8446 §4.4.2 requires a server that demands client authentication to abort with a fatal alert. The library's own server example shows the bypassable pattern: `examples/tlshelloworld.c:156` requests a client certificate and `:179` gates only on `tls_established(context) == 1`.\n\n**2. `tls_client_verified()` reports success for a certificate that was never validated.** The accessor is:\n\n```c title=tlse.c startLineNumber=12980 endLineNumber=12984\nint tls_client_verified(struct TLSContext *context) {\n    if ((!context) || (context->critical_error))\n        return 0;\n\n    return (context->client_verified == 1);\n}\n```\n\n`client_verified` is set to 1 only by `tls_parse_verify_tls13()` (`tlse.c:9450-9451`) and `tls_parse_verify()` (`tlse.c:9525-9527`), after the CertificateVerify signature checks out against the certificate the peer itself supplied. Nothing on the handshake path validates that certificate against a trust anchor: `tls_parse_certificate()` parses the DER and stores the chain (`tlse.c:8749-8753`) with no issuer or chain check, and the internal chain validators are never called from the handshake — they run only if the application installs `tls_default_verify` as its callback. A NULL callback is explicitly supported (\"If the other side just presented a certificate and certificate_verify is not NULL, it will be called\", `tlse.h:337-340`), so the default posture is fail-open and `tls_client_verified()` returns 1 for a certificate the attacker minted seconds earlier. Both defects reproduce on TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3 (and DTLS shares the same handler). The snapshot has one squashed commit (\"Fix for #164\"), so no line history is available.",
              "remediation_steps": "Enforce client authentication instead of requesting it. In the server branch of the Certificate handler, when the application asked for a client certificate, abort the handshake with a fatal alert if the peer presented an empty certificate list, in both the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.0-1.2 paths, as RFC 8446 section 4.4.2 requires. Make the default fail closed when a client certificate was requested but no verification callback was supplied, so a peer cannot be reported as verified against a certificate the library never validated; applications that intentionally accept arbitrary certificates can still supply an explicit callback. Document that `tls_client_verified()` reflects proof of possession of the presented key rather than validation against a trust anchor, and correct `examples/tlshelloworld.c` so it gates on both `tls_established()` and `tls_client_verified()` and performs real chain validation in its `verify_signature` callback instead of returning `no_error` unconditionally.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that client-certificate trust is conventionally application-supplied: the library exposes `tls_certificate_chain_is_valid*` and accepts a `certificate_verify` callback, and an application that installs `tls_default_verify` with loaded roots does reject an untrusted client certificate — verified directly, the same PoC with `tls_default_verify` installed aborts the handshake for the self-signed peer. That control, however, does not cover the more important path: it is invoked only when `client_certificates_count > 0`, so a peer that sends an empty certificate list is never subjected to it and the handshake completes no matter which callback is installed — tested with `tls_default_verify` and with a permissive callback, both with an empty list. Nothing in the library or README states that `tls_client_verified()` means proof of possession rather than validated identity, and the accessor returns 1 in the default callback-less configuration, which is a fail-open default rather than a documented policy. The shipped server example gates on `tls_established()` while requesting a client certificate, demonstrating the bypassable pattern in first-party code. What lowers severity is that deployments not relying on client certificates are unaffected, and that scenario B is neutralised by an application-supplied verification callback.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Reproduced end to end against an unmodified build of this source on TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, with a working PoC, a gate matrix, a positive control for the legitimate client path, and a rebuilt patched binary that closes both scenarios. The only untested variation is DTLS, which shares the same Certificate handler but was not exercised end to end.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "If the deployment only gates non-sensitive resources on client certificates, the confidentiality metric drops to L and the finding becomes medium; conversely, confirmation that a production server combines `tls_request_client_certificate()` with a `tls_established()`-based gate for administrative or tenant-isolated endpoints keeps this at high and could raise integrity impact, since the same unauthenticated peer can submit arbitrary requests the server treats as authenticated.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — security closure.** Both edits were applied to a copy of the snapshot and the PoC rebuilt against the patched tree. The certificate-less peer now ends with `RESULT ... established=0 ... granted=0`, and so does the self-signed peer (exit status 1 in both cases), versus `established=1 ... granted=1` before the patch. The whole matrix (TLS 1.3 and 1.2, gates `established` and `verified`, peers: none, self-signed, CA-signed) was re-run: every unauthenticated combination ends with `established=0`. The alert path executes as designed — the peer receives a fatal `handshake_failure` alert and `context->critical_error` is set, so `tls_established()` returns -1 and `tls_client_verified()` returns 0.\n\n**Gate 2 — bypass review.** Checked the sibling and alternate inputs: (a) empty certificate list with `tls_default_verify` installed — previously skipped the callback entirely, now rejected; (b) self-signed certificate with no callback — previously `client_verified=1`, now rejected; (c) the same peers over TLS 1.2 — the second location covers them identically, verified empirically; (d) DTLS uses the same Certificate handler and the new branch is not gated on `!context->dtls`, so it applies there too, though DTLS was not driven end to end (gap); (e) a peer that presents a certificate but omits CertificateVerify leaves `client_verified` at 0, which the accessor already reports correctly and the patch does not change. One case is deliberately not closed: an application that installs a callback returning `no_error` without validating still accepts arbitrary certificates — an explicit application decision, documented in remediation rather than blocked by the library.\n\n**Gate 3 — preserved behavior.** A legitimate client certificate signed by a CA in the loaded trust store, with `tls_default_verify` installed, still completes with `client_verified=1` and receives the resource in TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2, before and after the patch. Applications that never call `tls_request_client_certificate()` are untouched (all new conditions are guarded by `context->request_client_certificate`), and client-side branches are unchanged.\n\n**Gate 4 — repository checks.** The patched `tlse.c` compiles cleanly with `gcc -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -O2 -Wall -Wextra -c` (no diagnostics in the changed region; remaining warnings are pre-existing in the vendored `libtomcrypt.c`), and the AddressSanitizer/UBSan harness build runs both scenarios without memory errors. The repository ships no test suite, so there is no project test target to run. Narrowest-complete-change judgement: the presence check enforces the invariant on every server path; the `!certificate_verify` clause covers the same invariant (a \"verified\" verdict must follow a real validation decision) which the application cannot enforce after the fact.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Generate an RSA server certificate/key pair and an attacker certificate/key pair with `openssl` (`server_cert.pem`, `server_key.pem`, `attacker_cert.pem`, `attacker_key.pem`).\n2. Build the PoC against the library: `gcc -DTLS_AMALGAMATION -g -O0 -o poc poc_compact.c`.\n3. Scenario A — TLS 1.3 server calls `tls_request_client_certificate()`, installs no verification callback, and gates the protected resource on handshake completion; the client loads no certificate: `./poc server_cert.pem server_key.pem - - established`. The empty certificate list is accepted (`client_certificates=0`), no server-side callback runs, and the client reads `TOP-SECRET-DATA` (`granted=1`).\n4. Scenario B — same server, additionally gating on `tls_client_verified() == 1`, still without a callback; the client presents its own self-signed certificate: `./poc server_cert.pem server_key.pem attacker_cert.pem attacker_key.pem verified`. The handshake completes with `client_verified=1` and the resource is granted to a peer whose certificate chains to nothing.\n5. Repeat with a TLS 1.2 server (`tls_create_context(1, TLS_V12)`); both outcomes reproduce.\n6. Apply the remediation, rebuild, and re-run: both scenarios end with `established=0` and no data, while a legitimate CA-signed client certificate with `tls_default_verify` installed still completes with `client_verified=1`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-294",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "No DTLS anti-replay window: captured application-data records are re-delivered to the application indefinitely\n\nThe DTLS record layer keeps no anti-replay state, so an encrypted application-data record captured off the wire can be re-delivered to the server at any later time and is accepted again: `tls_read()` returns the same plaintext and the application acts on it a second time. The monotonic `tls_write` stream of a TLS connection does not apply to DTLS, and nothing in `tls_consume_stream()`/`tls_read()` tells the application that the record was a duplicate."
          },
