        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/der-parser-memory-safety-unbounded-recursion",
          "ruleIndex": 27,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "DER parser memory safety / unbounded recursion — No issue identified: x509.c DER/ASN.1 certificate parser (parse_x509_cert_relaxed, x509.c:11863)\n\nTested rather than read: built the flag configuration (-DTLS_FULL_X509, which is what includes x509.c) and fuzzed tls_certificate_parse with 40k mutated real certificates (RSA + EC seeds) under ASan/UBSan. No memory-safety fault, no crash. The parser's controls hold: get_length (x509.c:5630) rejects indefinite length 0x80, rejects non-minimal long forms, bounds every length against the remaining buffer, and every parse_* function re-checks adv_len/parsed before use. Two non-memory conclusions recorded instead: (1) TLS_FULL_X509 is not referenced by any build file and with the flag the TLSCertificate mapping leaves not_before/not_after/SAN/pk unset, so chain validation always fails closed (default_verify=42 for a valid chain) — x509.c is not the production parser; (2) that mapping leaks one buffer per parse (asn1_parse allocates cert->bytes at tlse.c:11359 and tls_certificate_parse overwrites the pointer at tlse.c:11797; measured 869 bytes leaked per certificate) — a low-impact unbounded-growth defect confined to that non-default configuration."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "x509.c DER/ASN.1 certificate parser (parse_x509_cert_relaxed, x509.c:11863)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "no_issue_found",
              "risk_area": "DER parser memory safety / unbounded recursion",
