        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/signature-verification-correctness-algorithm-downgrade",
          "ruleIndex": 33,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Signature verification correctness / algorithm downgrade — Ruled out: tls_verify_signed_data (tlse.c:11815)\n\nPositive and negative controls against real openssl-generated chains and signatures: tls_verify_signed_data accepts a correct RSA/SHA-256 signature (1) and rejects a signature over different data, a signature checked against the wrong certificate, an unknown OID (1.2.3.4), ecdsa-with-SHA256 with an RSA signer, an empty OID, an empty signature, and a valid SHA1 signature (policy=1 rejects MD5/SHA1 at tlse.c:11081). The algorithm comes only from the caller-supplied OID via tls_certificate_set_algorithm(), an unrecognised OID leaves algorithm 0 and _private_tls_hash_len() returns 0, which fails closed at tlse.c:11094. Signature is computed over the caller's data with that algorithm and verified with the signer's public key, so no path returns success without a verification."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_verify_signed_data (tlse.c:11815)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Signature verification correctness / algorithm downgrade",
              "surface": "tls_verify_signed_data (tlse.c:11815)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/hostname-san-matching-wildcards-embedded-nul-missing-san",
          "ruleIndex": 34,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hostname/SAN matching (wildcards, embedded NUL, missing SAN) — Ruled out: tls_certificate_valid_subject / tls_certificate_valid_subject_name / tls_default_verify subject check (tlse.c:4342-4510, 13534)\n\nNamed controls: (1) tls_default_verify refuses to check a subject when the leaf has no SAN (tlse.c:13544-13546), so a CN-only certificate cannot satisfy hostname matching; (2) _private_tls_certificate_add_subject_name rejects any SAN value containing a NUL or a byte above 127, so the NUL-terminated comparison cannot be truncated and non-ASCII SANs are dropped; (3) tls_certificate_valid_subject_name only honours a wildcard at position 0 followed by \".\" and compares the remainder against the first dot in the requested name with strcasecmp to end-of-string. Tested: example.com -> 255, www.example.com -> 255, EXAMPLE.COM -> 255, a.b.example.com -> 42, evil.com -> 42, example.com. (trailing dot) -> 42, certificate without SAN at all -> 42. IPv4/IPv6 SAN entries (0x87) are matched byte-wise against the parsed literal. No bypass found."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_valid_subject / tls_certificate_valid_subject_name / tls_default_verify subject check (tlse.c:4342-4510, 13534)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Hostname/SAN matching (wildcards, embedded NUL, missing SAN)",
              "surface": "tls_certificate_valid_subject / tls_certificate_valid_subject_name / tls_default_verify subject check (tlse.c:4342-4510, 13534)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/trust-anchor-binding-chain-building-weak-keys-expiry",
          "ruleIndex": 35,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Trust anchor binding, chain building, weak keys, expiry — Ruled out: tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root (tlse.c:11250-11306)\n\nTrust anchor checking works: with a root CA generated locally, the legitimate chain [leaf, int] returns chain_is_valid_root=0 and default_verify=no_error; loading only an unrelated root CA for the same chain returns 42 (bad_certificate) at tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root. A self-signed attacker certificate cannot be chained to a loaded anchor because _private_tls_certificate_verify_signature() must succeed against the anchor's public key (rsa_verify_hash_ex / ecc_verify_hash result is returned directly, 0 on any error). Certificates with a weak key are rejected by _private_tls_certificate_public_key_is_safe() (<2048-bit RSA, <224-bit EC). Expired leaf and expired intermediate were both rejected (the BFS only enqueues an issuer whose signature verified, and each dequeued certificate is date-checked before the anchor match)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root (tlse.c:11250-11306)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Trust anchor binding, chain building, weak keys, expiry",
              "surface": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root (tlse.c:11250-11306)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/validity-period-parsing-and-enforcement-utctime-generalizedtime-parse-failure-handling",
          "ruleIndex": 36,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Validity period parsing and enforcement (UTCTime/GeneralizedTime, parse failure handling) — No issue identified: tls_certificate_is_valid / tls_certificate_set_copy_date (tlse.c:4505-4542, 4560-4579)\n\nValidity comparison itself is sound but the stored string is wrong: tls_certificate_set_copy_date() allocates len+3, writes \"20\"/\"19\" then the UTCTime, and then writes the NUL at index len instead of len+2 (tlse.c:4575), so a UTCTime of 261117103627Z is stored as \"2026111710362\" — the last two characters are lost. Because the year/month/day/hour/minute digits are intact and strcasecmp stops at the truncation, both comparisons in tls_certificate_is_valid() stay monotonic: an expired certificate still compares as expired and a future notBefore still compares as future (granularity error is at most one digit of seconds, ~10s, and errs toward rejection). GeneralisedTime (tag 0x18) is not parsed at all, which makes not_after NULL and tls_certificate_is_valid() return certificate_unknown, i.e. fail closed. Checked that a parse failure can never be read as valid: every non-8-digit or absent date leaves the pointer NULL and tls_certificate_is_valid() rejects NULL dates at tlse.c:4515-4517."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_is_valid / tls_certificate_set_copy_date (tlse.c:4505-4542, 4560-4579)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "no_issue_found",
              "risk_area": "Validity period parsing and enforcement (UTCTime/GeneralizedTime, parse failure handling)",
              "surface": "tls_certificate_is_valid / tls_certificate_set_copy_date (tlse.c:4505-4542, 4560-4579)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/length-handling-nesting-depth-indefinite-huge-lengths-in-the-default-der-parser",
          "ruleIndex": 37,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Length handling, nesting depth, indefinite/huge lengths in the default DER parser — No issue identified: _private_asn1_parse / asn1_get_len (tlse.c:10891-10930, 11421-11762)\n\nReviewed _private_asn1_parse length handling directly: asn1_get_len (tlse.c:10891) bounds the long-form octet count against the remaining buffer, caps lengths at 0x00FFFFFF and returns octets=0 for indefinite length 0x80; the caller then checks octets<=4 and octets<=size-pos before advancing, and CHECK_SIZE(length, size-pos) before any use; pos += length cannot exceed size. The recursion is capped at TLS_ASN1_MAXLEVEL=0xFF with fields[] sized for exactly that. 200k mutation iterations of a real RSA certificate and 40k of an EC certificate produced no memory-safety fault other than the zero-length BIT STRING defect already filed as vuln-0001. Nested/indefinite/huge/negative-length INTEGER and BIT STRING forms, truncated buffers and duplicated chunks were all exercised."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "_private_asn1_parse / asn1_get_len (tlse.c:10891-10930, 11421-11762)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "no_issue_found",
              "risk_area": "Length handling, nesting depth, indefinite/huge lengths in the default DER parser",
              "surface": "_private_asn1_parse / asn1_get_len (tlse.c:10891-10930, 11421-11762)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/chain-trust-decision-does-the-public-chain-api-imply-trust",
          "ruleIndex": 38,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Chain trust decision (does the public chain API imply trust?) — No issue identified: tls_certificate_chain_is_valid (tlse.c:11212-11238)\n\ntls_certificate_chain_is_valid() deliberately does not consult trust anchors: it checks dates and verifies signatures along the supplied chain only, so a single self-signed certificate returns 0 (measured) and so does a two-certificate chain whose second member has no fingerprint (the `break` at tlse.c:11230). The default verification path is unaffected because tls_default_verify() calls tls_certificate_chain_is_valid_root(), which rejects the same self-signed certificate with 42. Recorded as an API-semantics trap worth documenting rather than a vulnerability: an application that relies on this function alone (as examples/tlslssimple.c does before also calling the rooted variant) would accept any self-signed certificate, but no library code path uses it as a trust decision."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid (tlse.c:11212-11238)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "no_issue_found",
              "risk_area": "Chain trust decision (does the public chain API imply trust?)",
              "surface": "tls_certificate_chain_is_valid (tlse.c:11212-11238)",
              "recorded_by": "X.509 &amp; ASN.1 Validation Auditor",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/trust-anchor-loading-from-pem",
          "ruleIndex": 39,
          "kind": "notApplicable",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Trust anchor loading from PEM — Not applicable: tls_load_root_certificates / _private_tls_pem_decode (tlse.c:13487-13528, 4257)\n\nNot attacker-controlled in the threat model: trust anchors come from operator-supplied PEM (root.pem bundled, or SSL_CTX_root_ca()). Review found the parse path requires version==2 (warning and skip otherwise), strips a private key if one appears in the PEM, and appends to context->root_certificates without a count limit (memory growth is bounded by the file the operator supplies). No input from the network reaches this function; recorded as out of the remote-attacker scope rather than tested for exploitation."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "tls_load_root_certificates / _private_tls_pem_decode (tlse.c:13487-13528, 4257)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
